WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.040
- Thank you for coming.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.320
My name is Yanjie Huang,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.510
I'm a PhD candidate at East
Asian Language and Cultures,

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:14.830
Columbia University.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.770
I'm primarily working on
modern content with China,

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:20.300
especially social and political history.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.350
So on behalf of myself
and my fellow panelists,

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:27.820
I want to thank Denise, Stephanie,
and the whole CAS teams,

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.440
for providing this
wonderful opportunity for us

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.160
to share our research.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:34.660
So without further ado,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.640
I'd like to introduce our
first speaker, Gavin Healy.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.630
So Gavin is also a PhD candidate

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.120
at Columbia University EALAC.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.120
He works primarily on the
tourist sector in Mao's China.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.480
- Good morning, thank you.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.412
Thank you, Yanjie.

00:00:54.412 --> 00:00:55.940
Thank you, Denise and Stephanie.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.470
I'm just gonna share my screen.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:02.360
So workers in China's service
sector during the Mao era

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.960
performed their jobs under
the guidance of campaigns

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.540
that attempted to define the political

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.713
and economic nature of service labor,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.980
but service workers, or fuwuyuan,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.330
working in hotels, restaurants,

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.940
and transportation facilities
that served overseas Chinese,

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.920
Hong Kong and Macau compatriots
and other foreign tourists

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.173
found themselves in a precarious position.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.080
Not only were they working in an industry

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.260
outside primary production,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.387
they were serving people

00:01:32.387 --> 00:01:35.703
who were not necessarily
considered the people.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.199
For these fuwuyuan,

00:01:38.199 --> 00:01:42.253
fuwu or service was a matter
of heightened sensitivity.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.000
They were portrayed as the
face of the new society

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.450
and were urged to perform
their work accordingly.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.453
As fuwuyuan as on the
Guangzhou-Shenzhen railway put it,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.270
when we do our work well, it
reflects well on the party.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.930
When we do our work poorly,

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.003
it damages the prestige of the party.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.200
This political element of fuwu

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.320
was cited in model worker narratives

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.090
as something that gave this non-productive

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.423
service work meaning,

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:15.250
yet striving for attentive service

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.520
and satisfying the
demands of foreign guests

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.410
were not always easy to reconcile

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:23.810
with political rhetoric that
emphasized serving the people

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.220
in a hierarchy of labor

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.403
that privileged agricultural
and industrial production.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:33.250
Over the course of the 1970s,

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:37.720
fuwu and its relationship
to tourism, China's economy,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.280
and the prestige of the socialist nation,

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:41.713
changed in many ways.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:46.130
Central to this was the
redefinition of the people

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.080
that fuwuyuan would be called to serve.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:51.210
As the relationship of the Chinese state

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.571
with the broader world transformed,

00:02:53.571 --> 00:02:56.110
fuwuyuan serve new types of guests

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.710
and found themselves
navigating new conceptions

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.310
of the proper way to serve them.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:04.700
Worker education campaigns
continued to emphasize

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.340
the importance of proper service attitude,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.280
but the meaning of tourism service changed

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.020
over the course of the decade

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.340
as it was interpreted
first through the lens

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.520
of revolutionary diplomacy

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.733
and then in terms of the
modernization of the Chinese state.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.379
In the early 1970s,

00:03:23.379 --> 00:03:25.883
fuwuyuan a pantry restaurant in Guangzhou,

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:30.070
discussed how prior to
the Cultural Revolution

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:33.810
they pursued feudalist, capitalist,
and revisionist service,

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.633
to a small class of bourgeoisie.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.240
With the start of the
Cultural Revolution, however,

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.270
they said they abandoned the
counter revolutionary line

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.080
of putting profit in command

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.113
and targeting both
refined and popular taste.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:52.210
Henceforth, they pledged to serve dishes

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.113
and dim sum for the masses.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.910
Other restaurants with a long
pre-revolutionary history

00:03:57.910 --> 00:03:59.940
pledged to do the same.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.960
In 1969, the revolutionary committee

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.970
of the Guangzhou restaurant

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:09.680
in a report on a struggle to
answer the fundamental question

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.880
of service for whom

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.150
likewise repudiated the
principle of targeting

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.453
both refined and popular taste.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.310
The committee asserted that
the answer to the question

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:25.100
of service for whom was
service to the proletariat,

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.210
and the restaurants perpetuation

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:30.910
of its pre-revolutionary
legacy of refined cuisine

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:32.023
was revisionism.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:34.540
In the past, they said,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.650
the line in the restaurant
between refined and popular

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:38.923
had been quite clear.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.440
When referring to refined and popular,

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:45.560
we considered the exploiting class refined

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:48.860
and considered the working people popular,

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:50.670
but now that sort of bourgeois rubbish

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.433
has been thoroughly eradicated.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:57.330
But 1971 saw two seemingly unrelated

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.560
but in fact closely
connected announcements

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.770
from China's highest leadership

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.600
that brought about a shift
in the conceptualization

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.197
of tourism service labor,

00:05:06.197 --> 00:05:09.973
and its relationship to
refined and popular tastes.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:15.950
In July 1971, Mao authorized
state tourism authorities

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.870
to increase the number of foreign tourists

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:19.660
allowed into China,

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.870
and to permit the entry of
not just political leftists

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.410
and foreign friends,

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:26.723
but self-financed writers as well.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:32.010
A month later, Chairman Mao's
revolutionary diplomatic line

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.433
was publicly articulated
in the People's Daily.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:38.320
The revolutionary diplomatic
line was the heading given

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.090
to a broad policy of
internationalist solidarity

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:42.533
against imperialists,

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.310
as well as solidarity with
workers and oppressed people

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:49.183
living within capitalist
and imperialist nations.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.990
Coming just weeks before the
PRC's submission to the UN,

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:56.650
the announcement of this
policy has been described

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:59.440
as part of the modernization or moderation

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:01.170
of earlier policies,

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.130
marking a consolidation of power

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.193
in the Foreign Ministry under Juan Lai.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.320
Yet while this policy seems a
little more than a restatement

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:11.220
of by then long standing pronouncements

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.550
of Afro-Asian solidarity,

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:16.263
it differs in at least
one important respect.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.210
The revolutionary diplomatic line

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:21.180
provided an ideological framework

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.610
to justify China's increasing engagement

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.683
with nations of the capitalist world.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:28.650
While just a few years earlier,

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:32.050
the People's Daily criticized
the revisionism apparent

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.773
in Soviet overtures
toward Western tourists.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.340
The revolutionary diplomatic line

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:41.520
separated the people from the nation,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.410
opening up space to serve Western tourists

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.820
while continuing to publicly repudiate

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:49.323
the neocolonial policies
of their governments.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.170
For China's tourism industry,

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:55.200
this meant a focus on
new types of customers

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.113
and new ways of
conceptualizing fuwu labor.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.510
In December 1971,

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:04.320
the revolutionary committee
of pantry restaurant

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.340
enunciated the role of fuwuyuan

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.962
in the revolutionary diplomatic line.

00:07:08.962 --> 00:07:12.420
The committee reported that
fuwuyuan must take the lead

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:14.190
in serving foreign guests,

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:16.840
must publicize Mao Zedong thought,

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.360
and be careful in carrying
out their fuwu work.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:23.200
They must understand the ways
of life of foreign guests,

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.710
and adjust their services

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:27.143
to suit foreign guests habits and customs.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:30.750
While they continued
to speak with contempt

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:34.510
for targeting both
refined and popular taste,

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.450
this was belied by the
attention paid to skill,

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:39.380
variety and refinement

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:41.830
when these could be linked
to the role of fuwuyuan

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:43.573
in revolutionary diplomacy.

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.600
When a group of Japanese
guests asked the restaurant

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.860
to prepare new varieties have been dim sum

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.530
every day for 30 days,

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:54.710
the chef said they consider this work

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:56.500
for the honor of the fatherland

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.323
and to win glory for Chairman Mao.

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:02.350
Their completion of this
culinary challenge, they said,

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:06.280
illustrated the material
abundance of socialist China.

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:10.110
These varieties of dim sum,
they said, exquisitely crafted,

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:12.570
gave the guests a deep impression

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:14.980
and showed how the chef's and fuwuyuan

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.160
wholeheartedly serve Chairman Mao's

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.993
revolutionary diplomatic line.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.150
Chefs from the Overseas
Chinese Hotel in Shanghai

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:25.960
similarly rethought notions

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:29.780
such as targeting refined
and popular tastes,

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.140
and emphasis on the
importance of skilled labor

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:34.420
when they spoke with the pride

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:36.490
of the compliments they
received from guests

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:37.340
on their cooking.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:41.710
This, they said, was
studying professional skill

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:43.293
for the sake of revolution.

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:47.390
In organizing a study group
to analyze customer's tastes

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.490
and develop new dishes,

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:52.250
they said they came to see more clearly

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:53.970
the political meaning of cooking

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:55.810
in the service of Chairman Mao's

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:57.933
revolutionary diplomatic line.

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:01.410
But their position at the vanguard

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:05.040
of the revolutionary diplomatic
line forced service workers

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:07.483
to confront a number of difficult issues.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:11.703
As the report from the
Shanghai Mansions Hotel noted,

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:14.490
fuwuyuan needed to draw clear lines

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:17.950
between popularization and simplification,

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:21.340
polite eagerness and obsequiousness,

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.673
actively serving foreign
guests and fawning over them,

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:27.940
learning skills for the sake of revolution

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:30.133
and placing skill above all else,

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:34.743
national dignity and arrogant
great nation chauvinism,

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.860
being mindful of poise and grace

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.790
and pursuing a bourgeois manner of dress,

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:45.050
and careful management of the
purchase and sale of goods

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.330
versus a capitalist emphasis

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:51.143
on material incentives
and profit above all else.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.140
It's evident that the lines
between these concepts

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.690
were not entirely clear to
fuwuyuan to at the time,

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:00.650
just as the lines between many of them

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:04.110
remain unclear in tourism
and other service industries

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.173
in China's post socialist world today.

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:10.940
Not long after the death of Mao in 1976,

00:10:12.435 --> 00:10:15.860
fuwu came to be linked to
the four modernizations.

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.590
The four modernizations:

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:20.060
development of agriculture, industry,

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:23.150
science and technology
and national defense,

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:25.220
originated in a report of Juan Lai

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:28.500
to the National People's Congress in 1975

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:30.850
and were written into
the Constitution in 1978.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.650
The skills necessary for fuwu work

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:37.350
in service of the four modernizations

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.320
increasingly resembled the appeal

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:41.890
to both refined and popular tastes

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:44.903
decried in the recent past.

00:10:45.911 --> 00:10:48.430
Fuwuyuan learned more types of cuisine,

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:49.760
the customs and tastes of guests

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.770
from a greater variety of nations,

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:54.770
and more exacting standards of
table settings and etiquette.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.890
Hotel and restaurant
workers studied new texts

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:02.020
like professional knowledge
for service workers,

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:04.510
shifting the focus of worker campaigns

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:07.390
from the clothes of the
70s and into the 80s

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:10.010
from mere valorization of fuwu labor

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:11.773
to professionalization of it.

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:14.860
Acknowledging the importance

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.480
of increased professionalization
of fuwu work.

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:20.430
Bo Zhuangzi, a Tsinghua news reporter

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.740
writing the People's Daily in 1979

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:26.590
cited projections that
over the next decade

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:30.570
more than 85% of new employment
would be in light industry,

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:32.713
handicraft and service related work.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:35.290
Referring to the steelworks

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:37.310
in the Bao Shan district of Shanghai,

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:41.490
it took seven years to build
and employed 30,000 workers.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:44.500
Bao noted the impossibility
of heavy industry alone

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:46.490
supplying the jobs necessary

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:49.033
for China's rapidly expanding workforce.

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:52.170
Bao said that with the
workforce projected to grow

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:56.880
by three million workers
a year from 1980 to 1985,

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:00.380
if heavy industry were the
sole source of new employment,

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:04.290
the nation would have to build
100 Bao steel works each year

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:07.080
to meet the demands of the labor market.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:10.100
For this reason, the
service light industries

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:11.700
and handicraft sectors

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:13.227
were seen as the main path to employment

00:12:13.227 --> 00:12:14.763
in the coming years.

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:18.490
Bao acknowledged that many still adhere

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:22.050
to what he termed the
traditional outlook in society

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:24.363
of looking down upon the service industry.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:26.610
While pointing out that many people

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:30.590
did not consider a fuwu work
to be an honorable occupation

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:31.743
serving the people,

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:35.020
Bao differed from
writers in previous years

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:37.320
who proposed ideological campaigns

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:40.290
as the means to improve
service worker morale,

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:43.200
and instead cited only
the influence of low wages

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.080
and lack of social welfare benefits

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:47.173
on the morale of service workers.

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:51.990
Tourism and tourism related fuwu

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:53.460
were still occasionally linked

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.203
to the revolutionary diplomatic
line as late as 1979,

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:00.430
but the objectives of tourism and fuwu

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:03.230
had clearly expanded along
with the growing prominence

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:06.063
of the four modernizations
and public discourse.

00:13:07.077 --> 00:13:11.250
Fuwu work was by this time
connected to a tourism industry

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:14.070
that was not merely
ancillary to political goals,

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:17.820
but whose development was
a goal in and of itself

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:20.453
as an integral part of
the four modernizations.

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:23.800
Urging fuwuyuan to abandon the notion

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:25.660
that fuwu work was menial,

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:28.550
instructional text for them linked fuwu

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:30.620
both to national development goals

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:31.950
and to broader definitions

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:34.000
of the concept of serving the people

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:38.400
asserting that service
to overseas Chinese,

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:41.870
Hong Kong and Macau
compatriots and foreign guests

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:44.910
is necessary for the
nation's foreign policy,

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:48.450
commercial intercourse,
international exchange,

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:51.240
and development of tourism industry,

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:54.670
all of which are in service,
socialist construction,

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:56.023
and service to the people.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:00.790
From the 12th Party Congress in 1982,

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:02.710
the four modernizations evolved

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:05.193
to new forms of political
and economic planning.

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:08.490
In respect of the tourism industry,

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:10.900
Vice Premier Zhou Enlai
set forth the principle

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:14.950
of friendship first,
with economic benefits.

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:18.380
As one state tourism industry
researcher interpreted it,

00:14:18.380 --> 00:14:21.040
friendship first, with economic benefits

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.590
reflected not the primacy
of one of these aspects

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:25.310
of tourism over the other,

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:27.600
but the intrinsic, unified relationship

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:29.300
of the political and the economic.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:34.000
Tourism, he said, was an activity
with economic attributes,

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:36.010
but it also carried political meaning

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:39.070
in its role and acquisition
of advanced technology

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.370
and management experience from abroad

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:44.880
and in it's promotion of
understanding and friendship

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.730
between the people of China
and the peoples of the world.

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:51.320
As the director of the Shanghai
tourism training school

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:53.350
put it in 1983,

00:14:53.350 --> 00:14:55.420
the goal set up in the tourism policy

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:58.800
of friendship first,
with economic benefits

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:02.693
was to reap bumper harvests in
both politics and economics.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.400
This was an acknowledgment, he said,

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:07.890
that in the past it was
wrong to settle accounts

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:12.140
in political terms alone
without considering economics,

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:16.100
but to pursue economic benefits
alone and ignore politics,

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:19.730
to ignore the political mission
of China's tourism industry

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:22.560
would harm the reputation
of China's tourism

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:24.623
and the reputation of the nation itself.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:28.200
Yet the roots of these
practical and discursive shifts

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:29.530
came much earlier,

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:31.340
and the announcement of Chairman Mao's

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:35.160
revolutionary diplomatic line in 1971

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.680
set the stage for the
commercial revolution

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:38.650
that would be carried out

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:40.980
in China's tourism service industry

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:43.413
over the course of the 1970s and beyond.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:45.103
Thank you.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:51.210
- Thank you, Gavin.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:54.650
So our second speaker, Peter E. Hamilton,

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:57.703
is a system professor at
Trinity College Dublin.

00:15:59.417 --> 00:16:02.080
He primarily works on
transnational networks

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:05.550
in between Mainland
China, Hong Kong and U.S.,

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:08.450
I think his first book has just come out,

00:16:08.450 --> 00:16:11.070
congratulations, Peter,

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:12.900
and he's currently
working on his new project

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:14.900
on origins of business schools in China.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:17.603
So, Peter, please.

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:20.523
- I'll go ahead and share my screen.

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:22.853
Okay.

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:26.940
I'd like to thank Yanjie Huang
for spearheading this panel,

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:30.380
all my fellow panelists for
sharing their excellent papers,

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.490
and both Denise Ho and the whole council

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:36.140
on East Asian studies for
sponsoring this virtual panel.

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:38.100
With professional
development opportunities

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:38.960
curtailed right now,

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:41.853
I really thank you for your
leadership and generosity.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:45.340
In 1991, nine universities

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:48.703
inaugurated China's first
permanent MBA programs.

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:53.870
By 2006, there would be 100
such programs in the PRC,

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:57.043
and 236 by 2015.

00:16:57.890 --> 00:16:58.730
On one level,

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:01.690
this explosive growth
testifies to a long history

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:04.000
of Chinese engagement with the discourse

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:07.000
of scientific management
over the 20th century,

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:10.060
which is the subject
of my new book project.

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:12.760
And this engagement took root
in the early republican era

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:15.150
through transnational
circulations of knowledge,

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:18.230
but had been derailed amid
the Great Leap Forward.

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:19.450
Viewed in this light,

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:21.580
the revival of management education

00:17:21.580 --> 00:17:24.150
represented a return to modernizing

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:27.683
and developmentalist practices
from before the Mao era.

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:30.090
On another level, though,

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:33.070
MBA programs seem to pose a paradox.

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:35.940
Although PRC officials have
long considered education

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:38.260
to be a politically sensitive area,

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:41.210
these programs were heavily
based on U.S. models

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:43.040
and often established in partnership

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:44.710
with American universities,

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:46.030
and even with funding

00:17:46.030 --> 00:17:49.350
from elite us educated overseas Chinese.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.600
In this light, the revival
of management education

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:53.980
looked a lot like state sanctioned

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:55.873
foreign and capitalist influence.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:59.270
Many scholars have examined
the role of foreign expertise

00:17:59.270 --> 00:18:00.300
in the reform era

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:02.750
and the evolution of
grassroots management practices

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:04.670
in factories and firms,

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:06.320
but historians have not really examined

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:09.530
the simultaneous revival
of management education

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:12.830
as a key component of
China's economic reforms.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:14.810
And so this paper
attempts to fill that gap

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:17.470
and contribute to both
scholarship on the reform era

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:19.820
and Sino-U.S. relations.

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:21.960
And so first, I will
discuss several initiatives

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:25.080
to revive management
education in the early 1980s

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:28.630
and the entwined discourse
of management as a science,

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:30.430
and then I will discuss the transformation

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:33.360
of management education
at Tsinghua and Fudan

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:35.630
over the late 1980s and 1990s,

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:37.230
which had a huge impact

00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:40.093
on all subsequent Chinese MBA programs.

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:43.860
So in the cultural revolutions wake,

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:47.840
a consistent theme and logic
among the diverse reformers

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:51.580
was refocusing the PRC's
industrial management practices

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:55.240
around new ideals of
efficiency and science.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:56.550
As a result, while scholars have studied

00:18:56.550 --> 00:19:00.060
the origins and debate of
macro economic policy shifts,

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:03.450
a more mundane but vital
aspect of this new era

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:06.950
was simply retraining ordinary
managers in both the party

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:08.850
and throughout state owned enterprises

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:11.193
in more professional
notions of management.

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:14.850
As a result, there was a
spate of ensuing initiatives

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:17.320
in the early 1980s and
government added industry

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:19.250
to research management science,

00:19:19.250 --> 00:19:22.603
and retrain ordinary cadres
and managers in its ways.

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:25.250
Can't really go into very many,

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:27.250
but one that is illustrative

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:29.440
was launched by the first
ministry of machine building

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:30.724
or Yijibu,

00:19:30.724 --> 00:19:33.330
and the Yijibu was one of
the single largest pieces

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:36.410
in the PRC's civilian
industrial sector then,

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:39.280
and also ran its own system of colleges.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:41.220
As such, the Yijibu had a vast

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:44.260
and really unwieldy
portfolio of responsibilities

00:19:44.260 --> 00:19:47.500
that could use more efficient
and better management.

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:48.950
As such, with political backing

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:51.210
from Bo Yibo and Liao Chengzhi,

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:53.050
in the fall of 1978 the Yijibu

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:55.820
hired a young MIT doctoral student

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:59.650
who's studying on exchange in
Beijing named Joseph Battat

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:03.330
to launch program in systems engineering.

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:06.240
Fluent in Mandarin and versed
in the political rhetoric,

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:08.880
Battat was able to tour
the Yijibu factories,

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:11.800
use his networks to purchase
materials and hire faculty,

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:12.970
and launch this program

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:15.570
at the Shanghai Institute
of Mechanical Engineering

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:17.680
in early 1979.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:19.950
And while termed systems engineering,

00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:21.760
in Battat's own words it was quote,

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:24.030
an MBA program in all but name

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:26.513
lacking only for instruction in finance.

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:30.160
Another set of management
science initiatives

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:33.090
emanated from the new Chinese
Academy of Social Sciences

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:36.140
and its Institute of Industrial Economics.

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:39.160
These included research
projects, a postgraduate program,

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:41.460
and even a national
distance learning course

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:44.023
known as the periodical university.

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:47.280
The Institute of Industrial Economics

00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:49.330
also formed the Chinese Association

00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:53.820
of Industrial Enterprise
Management Education in 1979,

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:55.820
which sponsored the
writing and publication

00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:57.550
of the first management textbook

00:20:57.550 --> 00:21:00.170
published since the Cultural Revolution.

00:21:00.170 --> 00:21:02.970
Written between March
1979 and August 1980,

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:06.900
by 27 economists and
scholars of management,

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:09.010
the textbook industrial
enterprise management

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:12.330
is really fascinating
on a number of levels.

00:21:12.330 --> 00:21:13.250
As well as a statement

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:15.460
about the revival of these disciplines,

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:19.610
this textbook also sought to
reconcile two key concepts:

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:23.310
Chinese socialism and
scientific management.

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:25.310
In very simple and direct language,

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:27.070
the texts repeatedly asserted

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:29.160
the future state owned enterprise managers

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:30.530
must understand quote,

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:32.950
that enterprise management is a science

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:35.040
and that the improvement of
China's management service

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:37.190
was quote, a necessary condition

00:21:37.190 --> 00:21:38.883
for socialist modernization.

00:21:39.750 --> 00:21:43.420
Simultaneously, the text
studiously avoided bourgeois terms

00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:44.720
such as profit,

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:46.670
and even marshaled Marx himself

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:48.730
to declare that large scale production

00:21:48.730 --> 00:21:52.190
would quote, inevitably
produce supervisory labor

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:53.453
and command labor.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:56.020
And chapter one in particular

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:58.750
does really heavy ideological lifting

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:01.510
to establish the crucial theoretical basis

00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:03.710
for the PRC to borrow safely

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:06.560
from capitalist management practices.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:09.810
In the 1950s, Soviet
and PRC management texts

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:12.530
always stressed that
economics was subordinate

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:13.690
to party leadership,

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:16.980
which is an obviously unscientific preset.

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:19.810
Now, chapter one put
forth an innovative theory

00:22:19.810 --> 00:22:23.293
of management's dual
nature, or shuangchong xing.

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:25.270
In this theory,

00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:28.590
which is visualized here
in this amazing chart

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:31.400
that I'm happy to discuss
further than the Q&amp;A,

00:22:31.400 --> 00:22:33.220
the writing group argued that capitalist

00:22:33.220 --> 00:22:35.930
and socialist systems of management

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:39.170
had but one common nature, or gong xing,

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:40.750
when it came to honing evermore

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:43.110
efficient forces of production

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:45.913
based on facts, experience and reason.

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:50.140
Thus in this realm, which is
on the left side of this chart,

00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:53.070
Chinese management could learn
from capitalist management

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:55.770
to boost productivity, drive innovation,

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:58.640
and deliver greater material rewards.

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:01.450
When it came to the social
relations of production

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:03.270
on the right of the chart,

00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:05.620
capitalist and socialist
systems of management

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:08.790
had distinct individual natures or gexing,

00:23:08.790 --> 00:23:10.650
that must be safeguarded.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.510
Here, there would be no borrowing

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:14.620
and this separation would ensure

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:16.410
that learning from capitalist methods

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:18.890
did not become exploitation.

00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:20.850
In particular, the texts
stressed the maintenance

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:23.460
of state ownership as crucial insurance

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:26.210
to preserve the correct social
relations of production.

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:28.950
And this creative justification

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:32.740
for injecting capitalist
management into Chinese socialism

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:34.330
encapsulates the intellectual work

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:36.590
that was being done by reformists,

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:39.140
particularly these academics
in the early 1980s,

00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:42.180
who repeatedly addressed the
Cultural Revolution head on,

00:23:42.180 --> 00:23:43.360
acknowledging that quote,

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:46.000
extreme leftism in enterprise management

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:46.833
had caused, quote,

00:23:46.833 --> 00:23:49.430
serious harm to the development
of management science

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:50.283
in our country.

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:52.690
In turn, the text insisted

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:54.950
that now there would be no conflict

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:58.170
between scientific
management and party policies

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:00.410
since in quote, normal times,

00:24:00.410 --> 00:24:02.540
party policies and state planning

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:05.700
always appeared to objective laws.

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:06.680
And so if this isn't clear,

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:07.760
the textbook is really trying

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:09.990
to reinvigorate management science

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:13.220
as an intellectual
bulwark against any return

00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:16.350
to the economic fantasies of the Mao era.

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:18.920
Simultaneously, the dual nature theory

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:20.930
seems to safely reassure everyone

00:24:20.930 --> 00:24:22.190
that there would be no changes

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:25.000
to the ownership of state firms.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:27.270
And so in short, the inevitable
conclusion of the textbook

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:29.080
was not just that scientific management

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:31.540
and Chinese socialism are compatible,

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:33.740
but rather that they
were an essential pairing

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:35.900
to achieve the four modernizations

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:38.730
and thus the textbook make
very explicit and concrete

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:41.290
some of the precise
intellectual work necessary

00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:43.810
to reconcile socialist ideologies

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:46.293
and increasingly capitalist practices.

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:50.510
So with the time remaining
I want to briefly highlight

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:52.470
how these kinds of initial efforts

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:55.740
to revive management education
on scientific grounds

00:24:55.740 --> 00:25:00.323
evolved toward American style
MBA programs in the 1990s.

00:25:00.323 --> 00:25:04.410
And the first formal MBA
program was established in 1984

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:06.740
at the Dalian University of Technology

00:25:06.740 --> 00:25:08.510
in partnership with SUNY.

00:25:08.510 --> 00:25:10.430
But this program did not last very long,

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:12.600
and unfortunately, I don't yet know why

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:15.350
'cause that research has been
derailed by the pandemic.

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:20.270
Future premier Zhu Rongji
was a driving force though

00:25:20.270 --> 00:25:23.620
in establishing Tsinghua School
of Economics and Management

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:25.370
also in 1984,

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:27.560
and he spoke often
throughout the late 1980s

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:30.800
of the urgent need to improve
China's management standards

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:31.893
through retraining.

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:35.060
Yet as Zhu's political career accelerated,

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:37.310
the actual administration of Tsinghua SEM

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:40.080
largely fell to his
colleague, Zhao Chunjun.

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:44.450
In 1986, Tsinghua employed
Zhao as the deputy dean,

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:47.380
and like Zhu, Zhao was an
electrical engineer by training

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:49.970
who had now pivoted towards
systems engineering,

00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:53.100
emphasizing again the
scientific aspirations

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:54.753
behind the revival of management.

00:25:55.784 --> 00:25:56.620
Yet in reality,

00:25:56.620 --> 00:25:59.470
management education at
every major PRC university

00:25:59.470 --> 00:26:01.360
remains small, under resourced,

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:03.580
and very intellectually constrained.

00:26:03.580 --> 00:26:04.920
At both Tsinghua and Fudan,

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:07.220
there was little to no dedicated space,

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:10.170
a faculty of primarily
mathematicians and engineers,

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:11.400
and teaching methods

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:14.970
that consisted primarily
of three hour lectures.

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:16.550
And so it was resolved by the late 1980s,

00:26:16.550 --> 00:26:19.740
both Zhu Rongji and Fudan Zhenlin,

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:21.570
were increasingly convinced of the need

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:24.270
for an intellectual
international partnership,

00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:26.470
particularly with top
U.S. business schools.

00:26:27.900 --> 00:26:29.750
As seen through Battat's program,

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:33.680
MIT had long been engaged in
such endeavors in the region,

00:26:33.680 --> 00:26:36.670
and was very interested in expanding

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:39.587
and was doing so throughout
the late 1980s and early 1990s.

00:26:39.587 --> 00:26:42.173
And by 1994, Sloan's leaders had decided

00:26:42.173 --> 00:26:45.900
that Tsinghua and Fudan were
the most promising institutions

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:48.570
in the PRC for a potential partnership

00:26:48.570 --> 00:26:51.640
and dispatched a team under
Associate Dean Alan White,

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:53.020
to investigate the two schools

00:26:53.020 --> 00:26:56.310
to try to decide between them.

00:26:56.310 --> 00:26:59.640
And even though there were
skeptics on both sides,

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.310
the enthusiastic supportive scholars

00:27:01.310 --> 00:27:04.810
such as physicist and
MIT graduate, Xie Xide,

00:27:04.810 --> 00:27:06.370
reportedly helped convince Sloan

00:27:06.370 --> 00:27:09.010
to actually partner with both institutions

00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:12.440
and the three schools signed
a memorandum of understanding

00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:14.223
in June 1995.

00:27:15.510 --> 00:27:18.320
And for Fudan and
Tsinghua the primary goals

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:21.330
of MIT China Management Education Project

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:23.310
were, quote, faculty development

00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:25.520
and the development of
cutting edge curricula

00:27:25.520 --> 00:27:28.773
for MBA students who will
operate internationally.

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:33.390
This cutting edge curricula
is particularly important

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:35.550
in marking the transition away

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:39.570
from overly quantitative
and scientific approaches

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:41.710
and toward one unthinkable courses

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:44.660
and things such as communications,
marketing, leadership,

00:27:44.660 --> 00:27:45.823
and other subjects.

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:49.270
And this is primarily
mapping on to China's drive

00:27:49.270 --> 00:27:51.700
towards the socialist market economy

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:52.620
and perhaps the justifications

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:57.120
of things like the dual
nature theory allowed them,

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:58.750
the leaders of the three schools,

00:27:58.750 --> 00:28:01.950
to put the political
differences between them aside.

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:04.950
And so the memorandum of
understanding makes no mention

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:06.800
of any particular political conflicts

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:10.830
except noting a few areas
that we put off limits

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:12.280
such as industrial relations.

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:17.950
This ability to ignore
politics was also enabled

00:28:17.950 --> 00:28:19.830
by where the funding came from,

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:23.440
primarily from elite
MIT alumni in Hong Kong,

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:27.280
a particular link with the
book that just came out,

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:29.330
Chinese American executive Shirley Young

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:32.630
had warned MIT against accepting any funds

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:33.970
from the Chinese government,

00:28:33.970 --> 00:28:36.910
and instead Alan White
have recruited tycoons

00:28:36.910 --> 00:28:39.803
such as these four to
bankroll this program.

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:42.860
These U.S. educated Hong Kong elites

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:45.090
also joined us projects advisory board

00:28:45.090 --> 00:28:47.400
and pushed the MIT China project

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:48.610
to focus on sponsoring

00:28:48.610 --> 00:28:52.040
trans-Pacific educational circulations.

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:53.715
From the fall of 1996,

00:28:53.715 --> 00:28:57.130
Fudan and Tsinghua dean's began
dispatching junior scholars

00:28:57.130 --> 00:28:59.550
to Cambridge for a term or a year

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:02.410
as international faculty fellows.

00:29:02.410 --> 00:29:05.190
And these sponsored exchanges
allowed Chinese professors

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:08.730
to improve their English,
studied MBA methodologies,

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:11.310
and prepared to teach many
of the exact same materials

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:12.823
in their own classrooms.

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:15.290
Since I'm running out of time,

00:29:15.290 --> 00:29:17.910
I will wrap up by
highlighting just two aspects

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:21.320
of the ensuing knowledge
circulations here.

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:24.810
First, this opportunity
was essentially mandatory

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:26.200
for Chinese faculty,

00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:29.650
and often included
wholesale career change.

00:29:29.650 --> 00:29:32.370
For example, June Qian Xiaojun

00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:35.950
made a huge disciplinary
shift from applied mathematics

00:29:35.950 --> 00:29:38.400
to managerial communications.

00:29:38.400 --> 00:29:39.810
She commented, quote,

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:42.600
I was in my second year
of teaching statistics

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:44.240
when Dean Zhao asked me to learn

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:47.190
to teach managerial communications.

00:29:47.190 --> 00:29:48.780
I wondered about taking on a subject

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:51.120
so far from my original field.

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:52.910
He said that no one at Tsinghua

00:29:52.910 --> 00:29:55.620
knew what of course and
communications should be,

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:59.250
but that MIT Sloan placed
great importance on it.

00:29:59.250 --> 00:30:01.330
The course needed to be taught in English

00:30:01.330 --> 00:30:02.760
and he thought I could do this

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:06.143
if I observed how it
was taught at MIT Sloan.

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:09.660
In essence, Qian's chance fluent English

00:30:09.660 --> 00:30:13.630
determines Xiao's decision
to reroute her entire career.

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:16.850
After her time at MIT in fall 1997,

00:30:16.850 --> 00:30:19.460
Qian not only returned to
teach managerial communications

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:20.910
from the same textbook,

00:30:20.910 --> 00:30:22.470
but also became a national leader

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:26.200
in developing this field
across Chinese universities.

00:30:26.200 --> 00:30:28.000
Indeed, second and finally,

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:30.490
the knowledge produced
by the MIT partnership

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:32.120
was rapidly disseminated

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:34.870
throughout all Chinese MBA programs.

00:30:34.870 --> 00:30:37.250
In fall 1994, the State Council

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:40.060
and the Ministry of Education
approved the formation

00:30:40.060 --> 00:30:43.480
of the national MBA education
supervisory committee

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:46.300
with Dean Zhao as its first chair.

00:30:46.300 --> 00:30:48.120
And the participants
basically, the leaders of this,

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:49.740
were worried that the rapid expansion

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:54.200
of MBA programs in China
would undermine their quality

00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:55.970
and thus the new national committee

00:30:55.970 --> 00:30:59.420
created a mandatory core MBA curriculum,

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:02.480
in which quote, roughly
half the nationally mandated

00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:05.960
or recommended core courses
aligned closely with,

00:31:05.960 --> 00:31:09.910
or are virtually identical
to the MIT Sloan courses.

00:31:09.910 --> 00:31:12.210
And so for example in 2005,

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:15.400
the committee began to
require all MBA programs

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:17.710
to teach managerial communications,

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:21.440
June Qian then compiled the
recommended course outline book

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:23.590
and to this day teaches an annual seminar

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:27.200
to train other scholars in
the relevant case studies.

00:31:27.200 --> 00:31:28.600
And another example I won't go into

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:30.857
but can talk about is
the course in textbook

00:31:30.857 --> 00:31:32.797
"Data, Models, and Decisions".

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:36.690
And so to wrap up this
paper and presentation,

00:31:36.690 --> 00:31:38.760
map out a two part transition

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:41.900
in Chinese management
education and ideologies

00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:44.520
over the 1980s and 1990s,

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:47.260
first away from Cultural
Revolution rhetoric

00:31:47.260 --> 00:31:50.170
and towards scientific ideals,

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:53.180
and then toward American
models MBA programs

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:55.840
through partnership with MIT.

00:31:55.840 --> 00:31:57.820
These shifts highlight that senior leaders

00:31:57.820 --> 00:32:00.870
were thinking very actively
about management education

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:03.730
as part of the economic reforms,

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:06.070
and the transnational networks and actors

00:32:06.070 --> 00:32:09.620
had real influence in shaping
the content and quality

00:32:09.620 --> 00:32:13.300
of Chinese higher education,
at least in this realm.

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:16.680
Perhaps most importantly,
focusing on management education

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:19.670
helps to illuminate and
make more he makes sense

00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:22.180
of the intellectual work
behind the reform era's

00:32:22.180 --> 00:32:26.520
blending of socialist ideologies
and capitalist practices.

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:27.353
Thank you.

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:30.800
- Thank you, Peter.

00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:33.543
So our third speaker is Adam Frost.

00:32:34.780 --> 00:32:36.080
Adam is a PhD candidate

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:38.227
in history department, Harvard University

00:32:38.227 --> 00:32:42.380
and he works primarily on entrepreneurship

00:32:42.380 --> 00:32:43.816
in socialist China.

00:32:43.816 --> 00:32:44.833
Adam, please.

00:32:45.810 --> 00:32:47.883
- [Adam] Hello, great talks.

00:32:47.883 --> 00:32:50.400
Yeah, so in my talk I'm
gonna be doing something

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:51.563
a little different,

00:32:52.460 --> 00:32:55.290
and I am going to be showing how I study

00:32:55.290 --> 00:32:59.360
this period from a more kind
of bottom up perspective.

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:01.660
So my research has been
motivated by what I see

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:03.320
is the central puzzle

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:05.450
of modern Chinese economic development,

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:08.450
namely, how is it that after three decades

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:11.060
of violently suppressing capitalism,

00:33:11.060 --> 00:33:14.450
the Chinese state was able
to transform its economy

00:33:14.450 --> 00:33:17.160
virtually overnight
into this engine dynamic

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:18.830
and sustained growth.

00:33:18.830 --> 00:33:20.620
I mean, according to the prevailing view

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:22.260
of Chinese development,

00:33:22.260 --> 00:33:23.860
prior to reform and opening up,

00:33:23.860 --> 00:33:26.500
the Chinese state presided
over a quote, unquote,

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:27.940
cellular economy,

00:33:27.940 --> 00:33:30.830
with highly atomized
production and exchange,

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:32.130
society was divided up

00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:34.990
into these self-reliant
administrative units

00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:37.280
like the commune, the city,

00:33:37.280 --> 00:33:39.320
labor markets were abolished,

00:33:39.320 --> 00:33:41.850
and the state erected
all kinds of barriers

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:43.390
to mobility and exchange

00:33:43.390 --> 00:33:45.750
such as the household registration system.

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:48.910
And as the China sociologist, Marty Whyte,

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:53.450
very recently put it in
terms of the movement

00:33:53.450 --> 00:33:55.010
of people, goods, and services,

00:33:55.010 --> 00:33:58.370
China in 1978, at the beginning
of reform and opening up,

00:33:58.370 --> 00:34:02.400
was a less integrated
economy than in 1949.

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:04.380
But here's the problem as I see it.

00:34:04.380 --> 00:34:06.060
If the Chinese socialist economy

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:09.870
were truly atomized as late as 1978,

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:14.870
whence came the sudden and
powerful entrepreneurial impulse?

00:34:16.160 --> 00:34:19.680
So in my dissertation and
in this paper in particular,

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:22.970
I develop and analyze original data sets

00:34:22.970 --> 00:34:24.790
to argue that socialist China

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:28.050
was not, in fact, void of
entrepreneurial activity.

00:34:28.050 --> 00:34:29.980
I show that beneath the veneer

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:31.490
of the formal socialist economy,

00:34:31.490 --> 00:34:34.880
there is this dynamic
realm of economic activity

00:34:34.880 --> 00:34:38.840
where entrepreneurs are
actively filling in holes,

00:34:38.840 --> 00:34:41.670
profiting from inefficiencies
in the planned economy,

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:44.143
and more generally,
just making things work,

00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:48.360
and specifically, I use
new empirical evidence

00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:51.180
that I myself created or assembled

00:34:51.180 --> 00:34:54.760
to advance the following
three propositions:

00:34:54.760 --> 00:34:57.380
I argue that the Chinese socialist state

00:34:57.380 --> 00:34:59.790
was never that successful

00:34:59.790 --> 00:35:02.590
in suppressing private
entrepreneurial activity,

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:05.410
that entrepreneurship
under Chinese socialism

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:08.060
was both greater in scale and scope

00:35:08.060 --> 00:35:10.830
than previous scholarship
would lead us to believe,

00:35:10.830 --> 00:35:15.530
and that the central and local
governments were not unified

00:35:15.530 --> 00:35:19.493
in their efforts to enforce
formal socialist institutions

00:35:19.493 --> 00:35:22.533
and suppress private activity.

00:35:25.130 --> 00:35:26.683
Sorry here.

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:30.780
So the reason why existing scholarship

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:33.840
has sort of gotten mischaracterized,

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:37.260
socialist China, I think, has
to do with the overreliance

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:41.100
on high level policy documents
and official statistics.

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:44.700
I mean, these sources at best
suffer from extreme biases,

00:35:44.700 --> 00:35:47.440
and at worst are really political fictions

00:35:47.440 --> 00:35:50.750
that are meant to advance state power.

00:35:50.750 --> 00:35:53.390
So in order to overcome the
limitations of these sources,

00:35:53.390 --> 00:35:56.636
I instead turn to grassroots data.

00:35:56.636 --> 00:35:58.300
And to explain how I'm doing this,

00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:00.240
let me tell you a quick story.

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:03.270
So one day I was doing
research in the city of Yiwu

00:36:03.270 --> 00:36:06.750
and I was visiting a flea
market on the outskirts of town.

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:08.660
And while walking from stall to stall

00:36:08.660 --> 00:36:11.150
and I was kind of digging through

00:36:11.150 --> 00:36:14.010
different kinds of socialist
memorabilia, Mao badges,

00:36:14.010 --> 00:36:17.120
and ration coupons and stuff like that,

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:21.810
I came across this big stack
of brown jacketed dossiers.

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:23.680
So I pick up the folder on top of the pile

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:25.990
and I begin flipping through the contents

00:36:25.990 --> 00:36:28.430
and inside were government reports

00:36:28.430 --> 00:36:31.690
written during the Cultural
Revolution on black markets,

00:36:31.690 --> 00:36:33.210
underground factories,

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:37.290
and other quote, unquote,
spontaneous capitalist activities.

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:39.880
So I got really excited
when I saw these documents

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:43.200
and I bought them all
without haggling very much,

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:45.170
and I asked the vendor if he had more

00:36:45.170 --> 00:36:47.990
so he shared with me his WeChat info

00:36:47.990 --> 00:36:50.630
and then he invited me back
to his home in Angwhei.

00:36:50.630 --> 00:36:53.000
So the next week I went to his home

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:56.060
at this little apartment
at the edge of town,

00:36:56.060 --> 00:37:00.210
and inside the apartment were
just pillars of documents

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:03.300
stacked from floor to ceiling,

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:05.660
except for this tiny
space in the living room

00:37:05.660 --> 00:37:08.500
that had been carved out for a tea table.

00:37:08.500 --> 00:37:11.290
The rest of the apartment was chock full

00:37:11.290 --> 00:37:12.480
of historical materials,

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:15.940
including former government archives

00:37:15.940 --> 00:37:19.200
still in their brown paper jackets.

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:22.840
So I ended up procuring
some of these materials

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:24.010
and for the next five years,

00:37:24.010 --> 00:37:27.360
I networked with communities
of document merchants

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:31.830
from whom I assembled
thousands of documents

00:37:31.830 --> 00:37:35.203
related to underground
capitalism and socialist China.

00:37:36.500 --> 00:37:38.180
So in the course of
procuring these documents,

00:37:38.180 --> 00:37:39.750
I was fortunate enough to come by

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:42.910
two exceptionally complete collections

00:37:42.910 --> 00:37:45.820
of more than 2500 cases

00:37:45.820 --> 00:37:50.820
of so called speculation and
profiteering, touji he baoli,

00:37:52.690 --> 00:37:56.110
prosecuted by two local
administrations in China.

00:37:56.110 --> 00:37:58.660
So here's just a picture
of some of the documents

00:37:58.660 --> 00:38:00.210
that belong to that collection,

00:38:02.090 --> 00:38:04.690
and each case focuses on the investigation

00:38:04.690 --> 00:38:05.840
of people like these men

00:38:05.840 --> 00:38:09.370
who were arrested for
engaging in various types

00:38:09.370 --> 00:38:12.300
of illicit commerce and
underground production,

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:15.800
in this case, they're
selling coupons and things,

00:38:15.800 --> 00:38:17.640
ration coupons.

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:20.780
So the cases involved all manner of goods

00:38:20.780 --> 00:38:24.940
from poached furs, to
smuggled wristwatches,

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:27.253
to arms and munitions,

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:33.130
and most importantly for me
and the purposes of my work,

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:37.030
most included some kind
of case summary like this

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:39.520
that outline the type
of illicit activities,

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:40.820
where and when they occurred,

00:38:40.820 --> 00:38:43.370
and what types of goods were involved.

00:38:43.370 --> 00:38:47.690
Now, in addition to
examining many of these cases

00:38:47.690 --> 00:38:49.620
in meticulous detail,

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:51.680
I also try to view them from a distance,

00:38:51.680 --> 00:38:54.730
I abstract their contents into data

00:38:54.730 --> 00:38:58.900
and then I use this data to
build micro economic data sets.

00:38:58.900 --> 00:39:01.580
So to explain how let
me use this case summary

00:39:01.580 --> 00:39:03.640
as a quick illustration.

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:06.370
So the case summaries provide
all types of information

00:39:06.370 --> 00:39:08.563
that are useful to generate data.

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:11.580
So in the first line of text here,

00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:14.330
we are told that the prosecuting
individual is a male,

00:39:14.330 --> 00:39:18.070
56 years of age, born into
a poor peasant household,

00:39:18.070 --> 00:39:21.780
and he's a member of
the Bai Peoples Commune.

00:39:21.780 --> 00:39:23.240
So we can code this information

00:39:23.240 --> 00:39:26.490
into categories like
gender, age, class status,

00:39:26.490 --> 00:39:28.880
and institutional affiliation.

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:29.780
From the second paragraph,

00:39:29.780 --> 00:39:31.120
we learn that the individual

00:39:31.120 --> 00:39:33.810
between the years of 1973 and 1976

00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:38.810
sold six head of cow
for a total of 1127 RMB

00:39:40.260 --> 00:39:42.410
so we can similarly code this data.

00:39:42.410 --> 00:39:44.660
And then finally in the third paragraph,

00:39:44.660 --> 00:39:48.620
we learn that he was punished
with a fine of 500 RMB.

00:39:48.620 --> 00:39:50.210
So from this type of information,

00:39:50.210 --> 00:39:53.260
we can derive the proportional
level of punishment.

00:39:53.260 --> 00:39:56.160
So we can take that 500 RMB

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:59.690
divided by the total case value, 1127.

00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:04.250
So you get the economic fine
in this case was equal to 44%

00:40:04.250 --> 00:40:07.040
of the total reported case value.

00:40:07.040 --> 00:40:08.880
And although it's not in this example,

00:40:08.880 --> 00:40:11.800
many cases also include information

00:40:11.800 --> 00:40:16.100
about the origin and terminus
of illicit economic activity

00:40:16.100 --> 00:40:18.193
that we can code into geospatial data.

00:40:19.670 --> 00:40:23.300
So over the course of almost two years

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:27.890
I went through all 2600
cases in my data set,

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:28.930
this particular data set,

00:40:28.930 --> 00:40:31.120
and manually coded their contents

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:34.500
into something that looks like this

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:38.420
and use them to create what I believe

00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:40.170
are the largest data sets

00:40:40.170 --> 00:40:43.640
on illicit economic
activity in socialist China.

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:46.560
There were all sorts of
challenges in this process

00:40:46.560 --> 00:40:49.573
dealing with missing
data, incomplete data,

00:40:50.570 --> 00:40:51.920
and I won't go into that here

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:54.090
but I'm happy to talk
more about that in the Q&amp;A

00:40:54.090 --> 00:40:56.040
if anyone's interested.

00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:58.253
So what does the data tell us?

00:40:59.520 --> 00:41:01.820
First, the data set reveals
something about the scale

00:41:01.820 --> 00:41:04.410
of these illicit economic activities.

00:41:04.410 --> 00:41:06.320
If we look at the summary statistics

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:09.680
for the two counties that
I have analyzed here,

00:41:09.680 --> 00:41:11.750
we find that the mean value of goods

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:16.303
described in the cases was on
the order of three to 500 RMB.

00:41:17.290 --> 00:41:22.290
Moreover, the average amount
of cash seized in the cases

00:41:22.710 --> 00:41:24.950
was about 100 RMB,

00:41:24.950 --> 00:41:28.150
so these are pretty huge
numbers for this period.

00:41:28.150 --> 00:41:29.440
To put those into perspective,

00:41:29.440 --> 00:41:32.680
the mean annual consumption
of a rural worker

00:41:32.680 --> 00:41:34.900
was about 100 RMB a year,

00:41:34.900 --> 00:41:38.680
and the annual disposable
income of urban work,

00:41:38.680 --> 00:41:40.420
I mean, was really a lot lower,

00:41:40.420 --> 00:41:43.040
still around 10 to 20 RMB per year.

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:45.440
So in other words, there's a lot of cash

00:41:45.440 --> 00:41:47.620
flowing in this illicit economy

00:41:47.620 --> 00:41:50.350
and the mean entrepreneur was not

00:41:50.350 --> 00:41:53.370
a farmer selling a couple
of chickens at the market.

00:41:53.370 --> 00:41:56.340
I mean, it's a specialized
producer a trader

00:41:56.340 --> 00:41:59.020
who is dealing in quantities of goods

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:02.660
whose value represent
multiple years of consumption

00:42:02.660 --> 00:42:03.723
of the mean worker.

00:42:04.890 --> 00:42:08.420
So the data set also provides
insight into the spatial scope

00:42:08.420 --> 00:42:10.660
of entrepreneurial activity.

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:13.630
So here, I represented
the spatial distribution

00:42:13.630 --> 00:42:16.560
of network connections
for the two counties

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:17.910
in this particular dataset.

00:42:18.910 --> 00:42:22.340
As we can see, the illicit
activity do not just occur

00:42:22.340 --> 00:42:24.270
within socialist administrative units,

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:27.480
but they go across them.

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:29.610
So there's a distinguishable core cluster

00:42:29.610 --> 00:42:31.420
from these two counties

00:42:31.420 --> 00:42:35.110
that reaches out to
more distal connections,

00:42:35.110 --> 00:42:37.350
places as far away as Sichuan in the West

00:42:37.350 --> 00:42:39.200
and Heilongjiang in the north.

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:41.220
And if we zoom in on the core cluster,

00:42:41.220 --> 00:42:44.500
we find that the network
extending from these two counties

00:42:44.500 --> 00:42:48.330
completely crosses provincial lines

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:51.270
and in no way really
conforms to prefectural

00:42:51.270 --> 00:42:52.713
or provincial boundaries.

00:42:53.580 --> 00:42:56.360
And just impressionistically
if we compare the results

00:42:56.360 --> 00:42:57.900
of this core cluster

00:42:57.900 --> 00:43:01.881
to say William Skinner's
classical delineation

00:43:01.881 --> 00:43:04.380
of physiographical macro regions,

00:43:04.380 --> 00:43:07.100
we find that the area of illicit networks

00:43:07.100 --> 00:43:08.450
that I've mapped out in these two counties

00:43:08.450 --> 00:43:12.840
conforms really well to the
Lower Yangzi a macro region.

00:43:12.840 --> 00:43:14.740
So from this type of spatial evidence,

00:43:14.740 --> 00:43:18.340
we can at least begin to
infer that economic activity

00:43:18.340 --> 00:43:21.490
was probably shaped more
by topographical features

00:43:21.490 --> 00:43:23.360
and traditional merchant networks

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:26.670
than by socialist
administrative boundaries.

00:43:26.670 --> 00:43:28.050
More work will need to be done here though

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:31.170
as I add in more counties
from different provinces

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:32.253
into my data set.

00:43:33.560 --> 00:43:35.900
But finally, and most
interestingly, I think,

00:43:35.900 --> 00:43:38.010
the data reveals that local governments

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:41.540
did not faithfully execute
central government directives

00:43:41.540 --> 00:43:44.550
for suppressing private
entrepreneurial activity.

00:43:44.550 --> 00:43:47.310
So in the 1960s, the central
government directives

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:49.620
basically stated that all goods

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:54.300
acquired through private
illicit activities

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:56.560
should be seized by government agencies,

00:43:56.560 --> 00:43:58.610
and that the severity of punishment

00:43:58.610 --> 00:44:01.640
for the convicted
speculators and profiteers

00:44:01.640 --> 00:44:05.730
should scale in accordance
with the aggregate value

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:07.770
of their illicit activity.

00:44:07.770 --> 00:44:09.260
In other words, the bigger the activity,

00:44:09.260 --> 00:44:10.560
the bigger the punishment.

00:44:11.500 --> 00:44:14.180
However, what we find in the data

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:15.290
is that the patterns of punishment

00:44:15.290 --> 00:44:17.570
were almost exactly the opposite.

00:44:17.570 --> 00:44:21.740
So if we divide the cases into
temporal cohorts like this,

00:44:21.740 --> 00:44:22.640
as I've done here,

00:44:22.640 --> 00:44:24.660
we noticed two really surprising things.

00:44:24.660 --> 00:44:29.390
First, we see that in the
sample only a handful of people

00:44:29.390 --> 00:44:32.660
received any form of
extra monetary punishment.

00:44:32.660 --> 00:44:34.950
In other words, when
individuals were caught

00:44:34.950 --> 00:44:36.630
engaging illicit activities,

00:44:36.630 --> 00:44:41.630
they were really only punished
by and large with fines.

00:44:41.690 --> 00:44:45.720
Very few people were sent
to jail, sent to labor camp,

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:48.653
or even like publicly criticized,

00:44:49.680 --> 00:44:50.790
and this was true

00:44:50.790 --> 00:44:53.343
even during the Cultural
Revolution period.

00:44:54.660 --> 00:44:56.880
Second, and equally surprising,

00:44:56.880 --> 00:44:59.540
even when entrepreneurs were punished

00:44:59.540 --> 00:45:01.890
with seizures and fines,

00:45:01.890 --> 00:45:04.920
the majority of goods were
actually returned to them

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:06.790
so you can see that in the
column percent returned.

00:45:06.790 --> 00:45:10.640
In every cohort, we have
more than half the goods,

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:12.410
half the value of the goods being returned

00:45:12.410 --> 00:45:13.660
to the entrepreneurs.

00:45:13.660 --> 00:45:14.710
So what's going on here?

00:45:14.710 --> 00:45:18.390
And if you read through the
individual cases as I've done,

00:45:18.390 --> 00:45:20.010
you start coming across

00:45:20.010 --> 00:45:23.230
the phrase yi gei ding
de jiage caigou a lie

00:45:23.230 --> 00:45:26.390
which literally means to
procure at a given price,

00:45:26.390 --> 00:45:28.360
turn into a price and procure it.

00:45:28.360 --> 00:45:31.500
And so what was happening was
that instead of seizing goods

00:45:31.500 --> 00:45:33.240
from entrepreneurs outright,

00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:36.160
government officials were
forcibly procuring them

00:45:36.160 --> 00:45:37.810
at low prices.

00:45:37.810 --> 00:45:39.980
But in effect, this meant
that the local governments

00:45:39.980 --> 00:45:41.960
were taxing the goods

00:45:41.960 --> 00:45:45.230
and then entrepreneurs were
being left with enough capital

00:45:45.230 --> 00:45:48.103
to effectively continue
their private pursuits.

00:45:49.010 --> 00:45:50.610
If we dig deeper into the data,

00:45:50.610 --> 00:45:53.120
we find that the size of seizure and fines

00:45:53.120 --> 00:45:55.410
was actually inversely proportional

00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:59.010
to the total value of
illicit economic activity.

00:45:59.010 --> 00:46:01.160
In other words, the bigger the activity,

00:46:01.160 --> 00:46:02.630
the lighter the punishment,

00:46:02.630 --> 00:46:06.290
and this directly contravenes
those central directives,

00:46:06.290 --> 00:46:10.900
and this was true in both of
my observed administrations.

00:46:10.900 --> 00:46:14.120
Moreover, punishments were not uniform

00:46:14.120 --> 00:46:15.520
as they were supposed to be.

00:46:16.510 --> 00:46:19.210
If you divide up the types of punishments

00:46:19.210 --> 00:46:21.830
based on various factors
as I've done here,

00:46:21.830 --> 00:46:25.440
we find that the illicit
exchange of ration coupons

00:46:25.440 --> 00:46:29.860
was far more likely to
be dealt with severely

00:46:29.860 --> 00:46:33.570
than the illicit exchange
of agricultural products

00:46:33.570 --> 00:46:35.490
or consumer goods.

00:46:35.490 --> 00:46:37.900
So this demonstrates that officials

00:46:37.900 --> 00:46:40.840
were being calculative and selective

00:46:40.840 --> 00:46:42.920
in their enforcement practices,

00:46:42.920 --> 00:46:46.360
they chose what types
of activities to punish

00:46:46.360 --> 00:46:48.533
and they chose which ones to tolerate.

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:52.290
And based on my individual cases,

00:46:52.290 --> 00:46:54.790
I really take this reading
of these individual cases,

00:46:54.790 --> 00:46:57.050
I take this to mean that local officials

00:46:57.050 --> 00:46:59.930
were really interested
in protecting activities

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:02.840
that they viewed as being
productive in the local economy

00:47:02.840 --> 00:47:06.000
while punishing those such
as ration coupon trading

00:47:06.000 --> 00:47:07.750
that they thought were really unproductive

00:47:07.750 --> 00:47:11.203
and frustrated their efforts
to move around resources.

00:47:12.370 --> 00:47:16.340
So, collectively, my new data, I think,

00:47:16.340 --> 00:47:18.717
demonstrates that elicit
entrepreneurial activity

00:47:18.717 --> 00:47:22.100
was a lot bigger and
a lot more permissible

00:47:22.100 --> 00:47:24.720
than previous scholarship suggests.

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:28.160
While formal economic institutions
during the socialist era

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:30.210
may have been explicitly designed

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:33.160
to divide and control the economy,

00:47:33.160 --> 00:47:35.400
socialist entrepreneurs
found all kinds of ways

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:38.010
to effectively circumvent
these institutions

00:47:38.010 --> 00:47:41.750
and help facilitate flows of
capital, labor, and knowledge,

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:45.930
and in doing so that even
gradually won the tacit support

00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:47.940
of local officials.

00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:50.910
So that was a very rough
kind of synopsis of the paper

00:47:50.910 --> 00:47:52.390
that I'm currently working on.

00:47:52.390 --> 00:47:54.580
I'm happy to go into detail later

00:47:54.580 --> 00:47:56.330
on anything that you
find more interesting,

00:47:56.330 --> 00:47:59.930
but I hope this at least gives
a flavor of how the sources

00:47:59.930 --> 00:48:03.800
and the methods that I'm
bringing to the table here

00:48:03.800 --> 00:48:07.950
are offering a different
vantage from which to understand

00:48:07.950 --> 00:48:09.670
socialist economic practice.

00:48:09.670 --> 00:48:11.130
Thank you very much.

00:48:13.410 --> 00:48:14.353
- Thank you, Adam.

00:48:15.224 --> 00:48:20.217
So the last presentation is
mine so where's my script.

00:48:22.611 --> 00:48:26.030
So my paper today sort of look at

00:48:26.030 --> 00:48:29.130
very similar question as Adam's,

00:48:29.130 --> 00:48:32.290
So I'm looking at the origins
of the Xiakang society

00:48:32.290 --> 00:48:34.130
to kind of show the kind of ideology

00:48:34.130 --> 00:48:39.130
that came after the decline of
Maoism in contemporary China.

00:48:39.300 --> 00:48:42.280
I'm looking at the burst
of the Xiakang society

00:48:47.910 --> 00:48:49.650
as a socioeconomic product

00:48:49.650 --> 00:48:52.283
of the Maoist Cultural Revolution.

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:55.490
So the central question of this paper is

00:48:55.490 --> 00:48:59.270
why did China abruptly
turn away from communism

00:48:59.270 --> 00:49:03.562
in the late 1970s and
never really turned back?

00:49:03.562 --> 00:49:06.420
What was the post-socialist new order?

00:49:06.420 --> 00:49:11.420
So what is the nature
of this Xiakang society

00:49:11.580 --> 00:49:13.710
that came after the communist order?

00:49:13.710 --> 00:49:15.870
And the why or the new order so durable,

00:49:15.870 --> 00:49:19.650
so even today Xi Jinping
he will still talking about

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:23.100
how China has fulfilled the primary goal

00:49:23.100 --> 00:49:24.283
of the Xiakang society.

00:49:25.730 --> 00:49:28.810
So the basic question is
about the historical dynamics

00:49:28.810 --> 00:49:30.850
that leads to the decline of communism

00:49:30.850 --> 00:49:32.793
and the rise of the Xiakang society.

00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:36.300
Previous scholarship have talked about it

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:40.040
from a variety of perspectives
like elite politics,

00:49:40.040 --> 00:49:42.720
how the Cultural Revolution
actually led to the downfall

00:49:42.720 --> 00:49:46.550
of communist system, the
rise of technocratic elites,

00:49:46.550 --> 00:49:49.850
China's shifting geopolitical
strategies in the Cold War,

00:49:49.850 --> 00:49:54.060
and as Adam shows, a bottom-up
source of entrepreneurship

00:49:54.060 --> 00:49:57.100
even during a Maoist era.

00:49:57.100 --> 00:50:00.300
So the perspective that
I'm proposing to examine is

00:50:01.501 --> 00:50:04.180
the dynamics where everyday life.

00:50:04.180 --> 00:50:08.890
So what kind of everyday
life dynamics were there?

00:50:08.890 --> 00:50:11.820
In particular, I'm looking
at family-state relations

00:50:11.820 --> 00:50:12.923
in urban China.

00:50:14.490 --> 00:50:16.920
So to understand family-state relationship

00:50:16.920 --> 00:50:18.920
in the Maoist China,

00:50:18.920 --> 00:50:23.420
I think is pivotal to
conceptualize this period

00:50:23.420 --> 00:50:25.640
as a period of what I call

00:50:25.640 --> 00:50:28.520
a period of revolutionary austerity.

00:50:28.520 --> 00:50:32.700
So basically, I'm talking about
this long period from 1962

00:50:32.700 --> 00:50:35.090
so basically after the
Great Leap Forward failure

00:50:35.090 --> 00:50:37.210
to the start of reform

00:50:37.210 --> 00:50:39.973
as so called period of
revolutionary austerity.

00:50:41.350 --> 00:50:43.460
So by revolution austerity

00:50:43.460 --> 00:50:47.697
basically means state-family
redistribution of income

00:50:47.697 --> 00:50:49.290
under the command economy.

00:50:49.290 --> 00:50:50.980
So basically, becomes state

00:50:50.980 --> 00:50:55.980
were forcefully redistributing
income, national income,

00:50:56.310 --> 00:51:00.525
from the livelihood and
reproduction of urban household

00:51:00.525 --> 00:51:03.850
to strategies like defense,

00:51:03.850 --> 00:51:08.850
and self fund constructions
and diplomatic spendings.

00:51:12.470 --> 00:51:16.720
So why was this austerity
a revolutionary austerity?

00:51:16.720 --> 00:51:20.430
First because it's a direct
result of the fiscal impact

00:51:20.430 --> 00:51:23.600
of the Great Leap Forward
and the Cultural Revolution,

00:51:23.600 --> 00:51:28.600
and then because the whole
mobilization of urban families

00:51:28.630 --> 00:51:31.340
to sacrifice for state
political objectives

00:51:31.340 --> 00:51:34.850
were mobilized and sustained
consistently by Cold War

00:51:34.850 --> 00:51:36.600
and ideological mobilizations,

00:51:36.600 --> 00:51:40.807
such as anti-revisionism and
the proletarian consolidation

00:51:43.650 --> 00:51:45.050
and the Cultural Revolution.

00:51:46.050 --> 00:51:47.960
So I divided into two phase,

00:51:47.960 --> 00:51:51.680
the first phase was basically
before the establishment

00:51:51.680 --> 00:51:53.550
of urban communes in 1968

00:51:53.550 --> 00:51:55.340
and second phase was basically the face

00:51:55.340 --> 00:51:57.853
of the sent-down movement.

00:51:59.750 --> 00:52:03.080
So the key measures of these austerities,

00:52:03.080 --> 00:52:04.410
I summarized it as four.

00:52:04.410 --> 00:52:08.670
So first, there is a
major urban wage freeze,

00:52:08.670 --> 00:52:11.460
which basically freeze
urban wage of all levels

00:52:11.460 --> 00:52:14.580
between 1963 and 1977,

00:52:14.580 --> 00:52:16.360
there was massive laying off

00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:18.410
and the job freeze for two periods,

00:52:18.410 --> 00:52:22.390
first, as a direct
result of credit default

00:52:22.390 --> 00:52:27.390
and then during the first phase
of the Cultural Revolution.

00:52:27.690 --> 00:52:29.560
And then there was massive rustication

00:52:29.560 --> 00:52:32.590
sending down people from the
cities to the countryside,

00:52:32.590 --> 00:52:36.010
first as a downsizing in the early 60s

00:52:36.010 --> 00:52:39.760
and then as the sent-down
movement in the 70s,

00:52:39.760 --> 00:52:42.250
and fourthly, the birth control

00:52:42.250 --> 00:52:44.340
which was introduced into urban China

00:52:44.340 --> 00:52:46.570
basically in the middle 60s.

00:52:46.570 --> 00:52:49.047
So all those measures
were forcefully imposed

00:52:49.047 --> 00:52:50.510
on the urban households

00:52:51.460 --> 00:52:55.340
so as to measure to save state spending

00:52:55.340 --> 00:52:58.693
on the urban households
livelihood and reproduction.

00:53:02.258 --> 00:53:05.490
So basically the physical rationales

00:53:05.490 --> 00:53:07.360
of the revolution austerity

00:53:07.360 --> 00:53:11.700
we can analyze it into
like two components,

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:14.080
the first component was economic cost.

00:53:14.080 --> 00:53:16.850
So basically, the state
was trying to force up

00:53:16.850 --> 00:53:19.160
on the urban household economic costs

00:53:19.160 --> 00:53:21.430
of failed political movement.

00:53:21.430 --> 00:53:23.640
First is definitely the Great Leap Forward

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:27.610
which cost up to 60%
of the budget in 1961,

00:53:27.610 --> 00:53:30.070
and then during the early
phase of Cultural Revolution,

00:53:30.070 --> 00:53:33.940
again, because of the
chaos that were produced

00:53:33.940 --> 00:53:36.260
in the first phase of Cultural Revolution,

00:53:36.260 --> 00:53:41.260
they were a lot of budgets
revenue amount to 25%

00:53:41.510 --> 00:53:44.537
in the year between 1966 and 1968.

00:53:44.537 --> 00:53:48.530
And then there was also the
more durable economic costs

00:53:48.530 --> 00:53:51.170
of China's Cold War mobilizations,

00:53:51.170 --> 00:53:52.620
especially during the 1970s

00:53:52.620 --> 00:53:54.790
when China was spending big amount

00:53:54.790 --> 00:53:58.020
on funding revolutionary
programs worldwide,

00:53:58.020 --> 00:54:00.630
on building up his military

00:54:00.630 --> 00:54:05.630
in anticipation of a huge
confrontation of the Soviet Union

00:54:06.170 --> 00:54:08.043
and building up The Third Front.

00:54:09.060 --> 00:54:11.000
So all those spending,
if you add together,

00:54:11.000 --> 00:54:16.000
it takes up almost half of the
national budget in the 1970s.

00:54:18.810 --> 00:54:21.590
So this is a graph that tells us a bit

00:54:21.590 --> 00:54:23.963
about the impact of
the political movement.

00:54:26.200 --> 00:54:29.583
So these are the revenues.

00:54:30.910 --> 00:54:34.310
So we can see that there was a big jump,

00:54:34.310 --> 00:54:36.900
first during the Great Leap
Forward and then a sharp fall,

00:54:36.900 --> 00:54:39.607
and then there is a bit of
recovery in the early 60s

00:54:39.607 --> 00:54:41.550
and then followed up another big fall

00:54:41.550 --> 00:54:42.900
in the Cultural Revolution.

00:54:46.780 --> 00:54:48.550
And this is the share of military budget

00:54:48.550 --> 00:54:52.020
in the national budget and GDP.

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:57.020
So we can see that after
the end of the war,

00:54:59.260 --> 00:55:03.390
it has been maintained at a
very high level of over 15%

00:55:03.390 --> 00:55:04.833
during much of his time,

00:55:06.230 --> 00:55:08.090
and then declined gradually

00:55:08.090 --> 00:55:11.813
force into like five
or 6% in the early 60s.

00:55:17.890 --> 00:55:21.080
But most importantly, I think,
we need to understand impact

00:55:21.080 --> 00:55:23.720
in terms of the supply of urban jobs

00:55:23.720 --> 00:55:25.540
because in common economy,

00:55:25.540 --> 00:55:29.960
urban jobs means a almost
a life long employment

00:55:29.960 --> 00:55:34.960
with a small basket of social security,

00:55:34.990 --> 00:55:38.937
and with guaranteed promotion of incomes

00:55:43.870 --> 00:55:47.710
as the workers get promoted
on their skill level.

00:55:47.710 --> 00:55:50.650
So, basically, we can see that
after the Great Leap Forward,

00:55:50.650 --> 00:55:55.270
there's a sharp fall marked by
the great the mass downsizing

00:55:55.270 --> 00:55:57.850
of urban workers to the countryside.

00:55:57.850 --> 00:56:02.130
And then again in the
late 60s and early 70s,

00:56:02.130 --> 00:56:03.480
there was another big fall.

00:56:05.600 --> 00:56:08.480
So, basically, there was a
huge shortfall of urban jobs

00:56:08.480 --> 00:56:11.530
during this period,
especially the early period,

00:56:11.530 --> 00:56:14.360
early period of the austerity,

00:56:14.360 --> 00:56:17.943
that only began to recover
a bit in the middle 70s.

00:56:21.810 --> 00:56:23.440
But talking about the austerity

00:56:23.440 --> 00:56:28.440
as this huge, big political
and economic project,

00:56:29.880 --> 00:56:32.210
we can perhaps create sent-down movement

00:56:32.210 --> 00:56:34.170
at the center of this whole process,

00:56:34.170 --> 00:56:37.760
because it epitomized the whole process

00:56:37.760 --> 00:56:39.410
in all its dimensions.

00:56:39.410 --> 00:56:41.080
So the sent-down policy in Shanghai,

00:56:41.080 --> 00:56:44.810
if we compare the scale
is about five to 8%

00:56:44.810 --> 00:56:45.643
of national total.

00:56:45.643 --> 00:56:48.210
So basically, Shanghai was
where there's a non policy

00:56:48.210 --> 00:56:50.503
was most rigorously implemented.

00:56:52.270 --> 00:56:55.390
In terms of fiscal impact
is a huge reduction

00:56:55.390 --> 00:56:57.630
in wage spending for the government.

00:56:57.630 --> 00:56:59.780
So basically, the
government can save huge,

00:57:01.320 --> 00:57:04.130
according to my estimation, 400 million,

00:57:04.130 --> 00:57:07.950
or 50% of the revenue
Shanghai during the 70s

00:57:07.950 --> 00:57:10.710
by just sending down employable workers,

00:57:10.710 --> 00:57:14.490
young student and used to the countryside

00:57:14.490 --> 00:57:17.880
so they need to feed themselves.

00:57:17.880 --> 00:57:20.290
It was also a part of the
Continuous Revolution,

00:57:20.290 --> 00:57:25.290
first as a continuation of
Pre-1949 CCP economic programs,

00:57:25.790 --> 00:57:30.380
like the grid construction
programs in Yangon.

00:57:30.380 --> 00:57:34.150
Is those are measures of
flattened urban rural wage gaps

00:57:34.150 --> 00:57:36.964
and it was measured to
preempt the bourgeoisization

00:57:36.964 --> 00:57:38.577
of the youth.

00:57:38.577 --> 00:57:41.580
And finally, it can also be viewed

00:57:41.580 --> 00:57:44.040
in the context of Cold
War as a war preparation

00:57:44.040 --> 00:57:47.500
against imperial powers,
especially Soviet Union,

00:57:47.500 --> 00:57:50.410
as a relocation of
manpower from the cities

00:57:50.410 --> 00:57:54.160
in anticipation of an air strike

00:57:54.160 --> 00:57:56.300
or other major war movement,

00:57:56.300 --> 00:57:59.600
and also, as an effort to staff

00:57:59.600 --> 00:58:02.500
the 12 huge frontier military farmers

00:58:02.500 --> 00:58:05.620
that are built up in the 1960s and 70s

00:58:05.620 --> 00:58:10.153
along China's Northwest,
Southwest, and Northeast frontiers.

00:58:11.160 --> 00:58:14.360
So these are the kind of slogans they used

00:58:14.360 --> 00:58:18.070
to inspire youth to
sacrifice for the country

00:58:18.910 --> 00:58:21.900
by going down to the countryside.

00:58:21.900 --> 00:58:25.150
So if we look at this issue
from families perspective,

00:58:25.150 --> 00:58:27.920
then we can find that sent-down movement

00:58:27.920 --> 00:58:31.250
at least add three types
of costs on the families,

00:58:31.250 --> 00:58:34.170
economic cost, first as
a cost of resettlement,

00:58:34.170 --> 00:58:37.450
because the family need to
afford for the basic equipment

00:58:37.450 --> 00:58:41.350
and sometimes even
foodstuff for the youngsters

00:58:41.350 --> 00:58:43.280
to go to the countryside.

00:58:43.280 --> 00:58:44.200
But more important,

00:58:44.200 --> 00:58:48.700
it is the cost comes up as a future loss,

00:58:48.700 --> 00:58:51.040
the loss of future urban job income

00:58:51.040 --> 00:58:54.530
because they basically lost
their guaranteed employment

00:58:54.530 --> 00:58:56.620
in big cities.

00:58:56.620 --> 00:58:58.390
And then it comes as emotional cost

00:58:58.390 --> 00:59:01.140
first as a pure pain of family separation,

00:59:01.140 --> 00:59:03.990
and then induced economic strains

00:59:03.990 --> 00:59:08.150
and the emotional cost at home.

00:59:08.150 --> 00:59:11.640
And lastly, it also came at
a longer reproductive cost

00:59:11.640 --> 00:59:13.850
because by separating the families,

00:59:13.850 --> 00:59:15.593
separating the generations,

00:59:17.010 --> 00:59:20.820
there's definitely a delay
in marriage and childbirth

00:59:20.820 --> 00:59:23.163
due to all sorts of uncertainties.

00:59:24.980 --> 00:59:29.060
So the case I'm presenting
today is the Hua family,

00:59:29.060 --> 00:59:31.300
basically a Hua Zhongmin family

00:59:31.300 --> 00:59:33.590
that I found really interesting

00:59:33.590 --> 00:59:35.300
from the Fudan Center
of Contemporary China

00:59:35.300 --> 00:59:36.810
Social Life Data and Research

00:59:38.878 --> 00:59:40.778
is one of the seven letter collections

00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:44.900
that I mobilized for
this research project,

00:59:44.900 --> 00:59:47.950
but the Hua family letter
came as really interesting

00:59:47.950 --> 00:59:50.960
because it represents Hua
Zhongmin's own letters

00:59:50.960 --> 00:59:52.000
to his daughters

00:59:52.880 --> 00:59:57.880
and other relatives during
this whole austerity period

01:00:02.170 --> 01:00:05.313
between the middle 60s to the late 70s.

01:00:06.900 --> 01:00:10.520
And Hua was actually
a very average worker,

01:00:10.520 --> 01:00:13.130
level four on skill level

01:00:13.130 --> 01:00:18.080
and working and earning
around 80 grand per month

01:00:18.080 --> 01:00:20.250
and his single breadwinner was his wife,

01:00:20.250 --> 01:00:25.250
been laid-off from the
neighborhood production teams

01:00:25.640 --> 01:00:27.980
after the Great Leap Forward,

01:00:27.980 --> 01:00:30.910
and then they had to
feed about four children,

01:00:30.910 --> 01:00:33.030
two of them were grown up daughters

01:00:33.030 --> 01:00:34.540
who were sent down to the countryside

01:00:34.540 --> 01:00:37.490
one Hua Zhongmin and another Ghiangshi

01:00:37.490 --> 01:00:40.143
in the late 1960s and early 70s.

01:00:41.360 --> 01:00:43.810
So these letters reflect
it on the everyday

01:00:43.810 --> 01:00:46.220
political and economic
concerns of the family

01:00:46.220 --> 01:00:49.500
and it also reflect the kind
of grassroots ideologies

01:00:49.500 --> 01:00:54.500
that we wanted then as the
basis of this Xiaokang society.

01:00:57.000 --> 01:00:59.690
So basically, from this
compilation of data

01:00:59.690 --> 01:01:00.810
from the letters,

01:01:00.810 --> 01:01:02.980
we can see how the sent-down movement

01:01:02.980 --> 01:01:05.000
during the first period of two years

01:01:05.000 --> 01:01:07.103
had a huge impact on family finance,

01:01:08.400 --> 01:01:11.300
whereas the income
because of the wage freeze

01:01:11.300 --> 01:01:14.073
was stationary at 85 grand per month.

01:01:20.220 --> 01:01:23.060
They had afford like 400 grand

01:01:23.060 --> 01:01:26.110
just to sent-down their
$2 in the first two years,

01:01:26.110 --> 01:01:29.740
so that's about five months income

01:01:29.740 --> 01:01:32.450
and also it represents
almost five to six years

01:01:32.450 --> 01:01:33.433
of the savings.

01:01:34.840 --> 01:01:36.650
So basically, they depleted the savings

01:01:36.650 --> 01:01:39.420
in order to just afford the initial stage

01:01:39.420 --> 01:01:40.523
of sent-down movement,

01:01:42.130 --> 01:01:44.110
and beyond that they need to borrow

01:01:44.110 --> 01:01:47.740
actually from the families
and also the work unit.

01:01:47.740 --> 01:01:50.240
So this is the kind of
family support networks

01:01:50.240 --> 01:01:53.410
where they collected the basic resources

01:01:56.025 --> 01:01:59.007
for the sent-down of the
two eldest daughters.

01:02:00.800 --> 01:02:03.410
So basically, they are
relying very heavily

01:02:03.410 --> 01:02:04.940
on both the work unit,

01:02:04.940 --> 01:02:08.913
which acted more like a
precautionary saving institutions,

01:02:10.480 --> 01:02:14.930
and moreover, they were
relying on the the families,

01:02:14.930 --> 01:02:18.293
the relatives, especially
the parents in laws.

01:02:21.280 --> 01:02:26.280
So how did Hua family situation
compare to other families?

01:02:26.320 --> 01:02:31.320
I selected like five families
from this archive of workers

01:02:32.780 --> 01:02:35.080
in the Shanghai United Front department.

01:02:35.080 --> 01:02:39.143
So these are five workers who
receive subsidies in 1973.

01:02:40.020 --> 01:02:42.390
And in fact, if we compare the situations

01:02:42.390 --> 01:02:45.370
between Huatining all those five workers,

01:02:45.370 --> 01:02:47.810
their situation was pretty similar.

01:02:47.810 --> 01:02:50.610
They all have like at least
one children being sent down

01:02:50.610 --> 01:02:53.720
and they were all like families,

01:02:53.720 --> 01:02:55.180
really just one breadwinner

01:02:56.578 --> 01:03:00.850
and you can see that but middle 70s

01:03:00.850 --> 01:03:05.850
they were basically barely
surviving under a meager income.

01:03:08.900 --> 01:03:10.390
This is their capita income,

01:03:10.390 --> 01:03:15.390
which is actually below the
stipulated average of 20 grand,

01:03:17.670 --> 01:03:22.670
and sometimes even below the 50 grand

01:03:22.840 --> 01:03:27.563
that was these basics
livelihood subsidy income.

01:03:30.530 --> 01:03:32.830
But the impact of the sent-down movement

01:03:32.830 --> 01:03:35.270
and austerity measures

01:03:35.270 --> 01:03:38.530
goes beyond just purely economic impact,

01:03:38.530 --> 01:03:41.760
it also impacted family
reproductive strategies.

01:03:41.760 --> 01:03:43.720
So basically, if Hua had his daughters,

01:03:43.720 --> 01:03:46.950
two of his daughters
actually delay the marriage,

01:03:46.950 --> 01:03:48.660
eventually married in Jeannine,

01:03:48.660 --> 01:03:53.480
but without ever returning
back to Shanghai until 1976.

01:03:56.780 --> 01:04:00.000
So basically, if we calculate
the whole family's spendings

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:03.440
and their reproductive strategies,

01:04:03.440 --> 01:04:06.960
then we can see that eventually by 1975,

01:04:06.960 --> 01:04:10.210
there were families economic
situations actually improved

01:04:10.210 --> 01:04:13.610
because there were fewer
children being born,

01:04:13.610 --> 01:04:16.400
almost no children being
born in the early 1970s

01:04:19.320 --> 01:04:22.060
and the family size just gets smaller.

01:04:22.060 --> 01:04:24.850
So if you compare to the
whole situation in Shanghai,

01:04:24.850 --> 01:04:28.820
then Hua family situation
was actually quite typical

01:04:28.820 --> 01:04:32.607
because if you compare the whole
situation in the early 60s,

01:04:33.570 --> 01:04:34.613
Shanghai was a economy

01:04:34.613 --> 01:04:39.613
with very high dependency ratio of 73.7,

01:04:40.740 --> 01:04:42.603
the fertility rate was almost four,

01:04:44.206 --> 01:04:47.110
and Engel coefficient was at 58.

01:04:47.110 --> 01:04:50.410
But by the late 70s,

01:04:50.410 --> 01:04:52.110
the dependency rate had fallen 1.5

01:04:54.105 --> 01:04:57.807
and it's actually kept falling
to 1.1 in the early 80s,

01:04:57.807 --> 01:05:00.460
but fertility rate was reduced to 1.2

01:05:00.460 --> 01:05:04.423
and Engel coefficient has
improved to about just 32.

01:05:06.480 --> 01:05:07.740
So basically what happened

01:05:07.740 --> 01:05:09.853
to the mindset of the people out there?

01:05:10.820 --> 01:05:13.330
So after years of those
economic frustrations

01:05:13.330 --> 01:05:14.543
and all those burdens,

01:05:15.580 --> 01:05:18.550
Hua Zhongmin began to realize
that what was the cost

01:05:18.550 --> 01:05:19.820
of the whole problem

01:05:19.820 --> 01:05:21.860
and to become the articulate those issues

01:05:21.860 --> 01:05:24.270
with his own friends.

01:05:24.270 --> 01:05:25.870
So as the economy was weak,

01:05:25.870 --> 01:05:27.720
he was talking about how economy

01:05:27.720 --> 01:05:31.577
and also his family economy
they failed in government

01:05:31.577 --> 01:05:33.720
they simply led people to bear the burden.

01:05:33.720 --> 01:05:36.950
The send-dow movement will leave
me in debt for eight years,

01:05:36.950 --> 01:05:39.910
I will try to pay off the
outstanding debt in 1976,

01:05:39.910 --> 01:05:41.147
rebuild my family economy

01:05:41.147 --> 01:05:43.937
and build a better-off
family, xiaokang zhijia.

01:05:43.937 --> 01:05:46.090
So xiaokang zhijia first appeared

01:05:46.090 --> 01:05:48.790
in all the letter says I have exempt

01:05:48.790 --> 01:05:53.080
at the sort of articulation of
a new vision of the families.

01:05:53.080 --> 01:05:53.990
But more importantly,

01:05:53.990 --> 01:05:57.160
it appears also in
grassroots opinion surveys.

01:05:57.160 --> 01:05:59.323
So if you look at survey of materials,

01:06:01.700 --> 01:06:05.440
80% of the survey sent-down used in 1978

01:06:07.700 --> 01:06:10.690
articulate that they felt a
sense of economic victimhood

01:06:10.690 --> 01:06:13.080
during this sent-down era.

01:06:13.080 --> 01:06:16.770
About 75 young workers
who were surveyed in 1980s

01:06:16.770 --> 01:06:19.440
during the Shanghai department
of propaganda survey

01:06:19.440 --> 01:06:21.990
articulated that they
regarded family welfare

01:06:21.990 --> 01:06:23.043
as a prime important,

01:06:24.220 --> 01:06:26.310
as more important than politics

01:06:26.310 --> 01:06:28.410
and all other concerns in the life

01:06:28.410 --> 01:06:30.500
and the few are much less
about class consciousness

01:06:30.500 --> 01:06:32.310
than the older generations

01:06:32.310 --> 01:06:36.620
during their own generations
experience in the 60s and 70s.

01:06:36.620 --> 01:06:38.090
Although to remind us,

01:06:38.090 --> 01:06:40.470
these generations were
actually the Rad children

01:06:40.470 --> 01:06:43.360
who in fact grew up in the high mountains

01:06:43.360 --> 01:06:46.310
in 1970s and the 60s,

01:06:46.310 --> 01:06:50.110
and yet they were also the
generation who rejected

01:06:50.110 --> 01:06:52.763
this whole conception
of class consciousness.

01:06:55.160 --> 01:06:56.250
And at the official level,

01:06:56.250 --> 01:06:58.520
we can see that there's
a new discourse of family

01:06:58.520 --> 01:07:00.340
as a foundational political order.

01:07:00.340 --> 01:07:02.780
So first Deng Xiaping's idea of Xiaokang

01:07:02.780 --> 01:07:04.900
as the goal of China's modernization.

01:07:04.900 --> 01:07:08.340
So basically, it reflects
what the grasses opinion

01:07:08.340 --> 01:07:11.330
has been calling for and articulating for

01:07:11.330 --> 01:07:14.860
almost five to four
years in the middle 70s.

01:07:14.860 --> 01:07:16.900
It also reflect well balanced articulation

01:07:16.900 --> 01:07:18.610
that each families sell,

01:07:18.610 --> 01:07:22.703
and basically the foundation
of the national body politics.

01:07:24.900 --> 01:07:29.830
So these are another two
lignite found family letters

01:07:29.830 --> 01:07:34.380
that shows what kind of political
opinions that people felt

01:07:34.380 --> 01:07:37.360
about the situations from
a Shanghai sent-down youth

01:07:37.360 --> 01:07:40.600
who were strangled in Anghuei,

01:07:40.600 --> 01:07:45.600
and basically he was promoted
to a factoring company,

01:07:45.700 --> 01:07:48.650
but never returned to
Shanghai until the middle 80s.

01:07:48.650 --> 01:07:51.970
So just you said he was
writing to his fiancee,

01:07:51.970 --> 01:07:53.270
political struggles are simply

01:07:53.270 --> 01:07:55.690
meaningless struggles for powers

01:07:55.690 --> 01:07:59.090
and the grassroots class
must have their own opinion

01:07:59.090 --> 01:08:02.940
of every issue based
our clear understanding

01:08:02.940 --> 01:08:04.850
of all self interest.

01:08:04.850 --> 01:08:07.150
And then I used to
consider myself a Marxist

01:08:07.150 --> 01:08:08.500
and indeed read Marx and Lenin,

01:08:08.500 --> 01:08:11.530
but since he are always
beyond the explanations.

01:08:11.530 --> 01:08:14.683
So after so many years of struggle,

01:08:15.810 --> 01:08:17.380
the conclusion that reached this act

01:08:17.380 --> 01:08:20.240
is actually that there are supreme ideal

01:08:20.240 --> 01:08:22.150
in an highest happiness in life

01:08:22.150 --> 01:08:24.270
is not the communist visions,

01:08:24.270 --> 01:08:27.580
but the witness in their
baby our best qualities.

01:08:27.580 --> 01:08:32.580
Let him realize our dreams and
stand tall among his peers.

01:08:32.940 --> 01:08:35.620
So we can see this kind
of competitive parenthood

01:08:35.620 --> 01:08:40.080
is already bubbling in
the grass root mindset

01:08:40.080 --> 01:08:42.170
by the late 70s.

01:08:42.170 --> 01:08:43.840
So what is the nature of Xiaokang society?

01:08:43.840 --> 01:08:46.450
First is a de-politicization,

01:08:46.450 --> 01:08:48.140
it means the end of mass politics

01:08:50.260 --> 01:08:53.910
as a consistent state
strategy and ideology,

01:08:53.910 --> 01:08:57.600
and then it also means a
more natural base of power.

01:08:57.600 --> 01:08:59.010
So the communist regime

01:08:59.010 --> 01:09:01.840
was no longer based on
communism and division,

01:09:01.840 --> 01:09:04.580
but actually effectively
based on parental care

01:09:04.580 --> 01:09:06.630
and the sacrifice for the children.

01:09:06.630 --> 01:09:09.590
It also means a new
ideology of development

01:09:09.590 --> 01:09:11.430
as a family wealth accumulation,

01:09:11.430 --> 01:09:12.960
so the family can share the wealth

01:09:12.960 --> 01:09:16.020
and can see the futures in
the national environment.

01:09:16.020 --> 01:09:18.630
And ultimately, it means
the family as a new unit

01:09:18.630 --> 01:09:20.620
of bottom up mobilizations

01:09:20.620 --> 01:09:22.400
through intergenerational sacrifice.

01:09:22.400 --> 01:09:25.970
So basically continues the
revolution was still alive,

01:09:25.970 --> 01:09:29.433
but only in terms of
economical mobilizations.

01:09:30.560 --> 01:09:34.540
So in conclusion, the paradox
of revolution austerity

01:09:34.540 --> 01:09:38.670
was that it basically ended
the Continuous Revolution

01:09:38.670 --> 01:09:39.973
as Mao has proposed,

01:09:40.810 --> 01:09:43.410
but at the same time it actually
transformed the family life

01:09:43.410 --> 01:09:45.390
and made urban families the new dynamo

01:09:45.390 --> 01:09:49.403
of economic mobilizations
in post-Mao China.

01:09:51.077 --> 01:09:51.910
At the same time,

01:09:51.910 --> 01:09:54.173
it transformed urban
family-state relations,

01:09:55.594 --> 01:09:58.830
from the kind of pure
dominations and absorptions

01:09:58.830 --> 01:10:00.350
into a class-based society

01:10:01.600 --> 01:10:03.470
to a situation where the state

01:10:03.470 --> 01:10:06.750
has to rely on mobilization
and sacrifice of families

01:10:06.750 --> 01:10:08.933
in order for its economic development,

01:10:10.690 --> 01:10:12.880
and finally I want to
highlight family letters

01:10:12.880 --> 01:10:15.981
as a key source for studying
this kind of relations.

01:10:15.981 --> 01:10:18.813
Thank you, and sorry for overtime.

01:10:22.260 --> 01:10:24.860
- Hi, so I'll go ahead
an introduce myself,

01:10:24.860 --> 01:10:26.900
my name is Denise Ho and I've been asked

01:10:26.900 --> 01:10:28.740
to serve as discussant today.

01:10:28.740 --> 01:10:30.400
So I will offer some brief remarks

01:10:30.400 --> 01:10:33.230
and then we'll open up to general Q&amp;A.

01:10:33.230 --> 01:10:35.700
So firstly, just thank
you for the panelists

01:10:35.700 --> 01:10:38.703
for including me in this
fascinating body of work.

01:10:39.600 --> 01:10:41.250
I think I speak for others in the field

01:10:41.250 --> 01:10:44.890
when I say that reading the
inspirational scholarship

01:10:44.890 --> 01:10:47.330
of the next generation
of leading historians

01:10:47.330 --> 01:10:49.460
is one of the most
rewarding parts of my job,

01:10:49.460 --> 01:10:52.663
so thank you for reminding
me why I do what I do.

01:10:54.270 --> 01:10:57.780
So as for the panelists in totality,

01:10:57.780 --> 01:10:59.930
I think we can address
three common themes,

01:10:59.930 --> 01:11:02.060
and the first theme is
going to be the largest,

01:11:02.060 --> 01:11:05.520
and the second I'll give
a bit less attention to.

01:11:05.520 --> 01:11:08.500
The first theme is that
the papers invite us

01:11:08.500 --> 01:11:10.800
to revisit the economic history

01:11:10.800 --> 01:11:14.850
of not only Mao's China and
afterward as the title suggests,

01:11:14.850 --> 01:11:18.030
but also this longer arc
of what the economy meant

01:11:18.030 --> 01:11:19.563
in modern Chinese history.

01:11:20.410 --> 01:11:22.620
Second, I think all of
the papers invite us

01:11:22.620 --> 01:11:25.360
to continue to pay attention to ideology

01:11:25.360 --> 01:11:28.330
in our analysis of contemporary China.

01:11:28.330 --> 01:11:30.570
And then finally, the papers remind us

01:11:30.570 --> 01:11:32.960
to draw on a diversity of sources

01:11:32.960 --> 01:11:35.080
and an increasing body of sources,

01:11:35.080 --> 01:11:37.010
and Adam and Yanjie in particular

01:11:37.010 --> 01:11:39.770
demonstrate what kind of work can be done

01:11:39.770 --> 01:11:41.383
with new source spaces.

01:11:42.260 --> 01:11:43.940
So first, the largest umbrella

01:11:43.940 --> 01:11:47.623
revisiting the economic
history of modern China.

01:11:49.190 --> 01:11:52.630
Under this, I have a first
category of economic experience

01:11:52.630 --> 01:11:55.510
and the context for economic experience.

01:11:55.510 --> 01:11:57.910
All four of the papers
are in their own way

01:11:57.910 --> 01:12:00.270
about overturning previous assumptions

01:12:00.270 --> 01:12:04.980
about economic activity
in the Mao era and after,

01:12:04.980 --> 01:12:08.170
or in the Mao era and its
transition to the reform era.

01:12:08.170 --> 01:12:09.760
Adam makes a convincing argument

01:12:09.760 --> 01:12:12.640
that entrepreneurship
persisted in socialist China,

01:12:12.640 --> 01:12:14.990
that it was far larger in
scale and more extensive

01:12:14.990 --> 01:12:17.710
than had been claimed to quantify it,

01:12:17.710 --> 01:12:19.500
it crossed administrative boundaries

01:12:19.500 --> 01:12:22.300
and it was tacitly permitted
by local officials,

01:12:22.300 --> 01:12:24.890
an attitude he suggests
was known to the people

01:12:24.890 --> 01:12:27.270
engaging in entrepreneurship.

01:12:27.270 --> 01:12:29.300
Gavin shows how the service industry

01:12:29.300 --> 01:12:31.270
persisted in Mao's China,

01:12:31.270 --> 01:12:35.360
though in the 1970s the very
definition of service or fuwu

01:12:35.360 --> 01:12:38.720
and the people it would serve
underwent a transformation.

01:12:38.720 --> 01:12:40.700
Additionally, tourist service pivoted

01:12:40.700 --> 01:12:43.410
from being an act of
revolutionary diplomacy

01:12:43.410 --> 01:12:46.020
to one of modernization.

01:12:46.020 --> 01:12:48.670
Yanjie's paper examines
how ordinary families

01:12:48.670 --> 01:12:50.650
experienced the financial burdens

01:12:50.650 --> 01:12:52.350
of the sent-down youth movement,

01:12:52.350 --> 01:12:55.070
showing how essential state policy

01:12:55.070 --> 01:12:57.730
resulted in shifting financial burdens

01:12:57.730 --> 01:12:59.550
from the state to the family,

01:12:59.550 --> 01:13:02.130
an economic cost that
created family debts,

01:13:02.130 --> 01:13:03.840
created family tensions,

01:13:03.840 --> 01:13:06.620
and ultimately lead to a turn

01:13:06.620 --> 01:13:10.660
from revolutionary rhetoric
towards a primary concern

01:13:10.660 --> 01:13:13.123
with a family's financial well being.

01:13:14.240 --> 01:13:15.113
And then finally,

01:13:15.113 --> 01:13:18.550
Peter's paper integrates a
history of management education,

01:13:18.550 --> 01:13:20.920
a history of overseas Chinese investment,

01:13:20.920 --> 01:13:24.790
and an analysis of how management
was theorized and taught

01:13:24.790 --> 01:13:28.480
to create links between
the Mao era and after

01:13:28.480 --> 01:13:33.380
and also between China,
or among China, Hong Kong,

01:13:33.380 --> 01:13:35.423
and North American business schools.

01:13:36.600 --> 01:13:38.080
Another category worth highlighting

01:13:38.080 --> 01:13:41.540
under this larger umbrella
of economic history

01:13:41.540 --> 01:13:43.173
is the role of geography.

01:13:44.750 --> 01:13:48.840
So each of the papers has a
different geographic lens,

01:13:48.840 --> 01:13:51.870
one that both widens and contracts.

01:13:51.870 --> 01:13:56.030
In his paper, Adam takes his
data and visualizes on a map

01:13:56.030 --> 01:13:59.650
showing how transactions
transcended geographic boundaries,

01:13:59.650 --> 01:14:02.340
and perhaps retraced
historic trading networks

01:14:02.340 --> 01:14:05.800
despite the Maoist ideal of autarky.

01:14:05.800 --> 01:14:08.340
Gavin's paper also has
a geographic dimension,

01:14:08.340 --> 01:14:10.590
and this aspect comes out even more so

01:14:10.590 --> 01:14:12.190
in his upcoming dissertation

01:14:12.190 --> 01:14:14.390
with its analysis of the railway workers

01:14:14.390 --> 01:14:17.510
who serve on the
Guangzhou-Shenzhen railway line.

01:14:17.510 --> 01:14:20.150
It is in this mobility
that his service workers

01:14:20.150 --> 01:14:22.020
sort out the meaning of service

01:14:22.020 --> 01:14:24.093
and the meaning of serving the people.

01:14:25.330 --> 01:14:28.310
While Yanjie's paper starts
and ends in Shanghai,

01:14:28.310 --> 01:14:30.510
he invites us to revisit our perceptions

01:14:30.510 --> 01:14:32.440
of the urban-rural divide.

01:14:32.440 --> 01:14:33.610
We could have conventionally

01:14:33.610 --> 01:14:35.690
thought of sent-down youth movement

01:14:35.690 --> 01:14:38.680
as one in which the state
offloads excess labor

01:14:38.680 --> 01:14:41.390
and puts the burden on the countryside,

01:14:41.390 --> 01:14:44.170
but here the burden is actually put back

01:14:44.170 --> 01:14:45.730
on the family in the city

01:14:45.730 --> 01:14:47.970
so it reframes our geographic thinking

01:14:47.970 --> 01:14:49.813
about the sent-down movement.

01:14:51.360 --> 01:14:54.130
And then finally, Peters
paper extends the analysis

01:14:54.130 --> 01:14:57.350
of his recently published
book on Trans Pacific Networks

01:14:57.350 --> 01:14:59.960
to this establishment of MBA programs

01:14:59.960 --> 01:15:01.390
by American universities

01:15:01.390 --> 01:15:04.963
and with the help of
elite Hong Kong graduates.

01:15:07.090 --> 01:15:09.540
Another category worth thinking about

01:15:09.540 --> 01:15:12.730
when we put all these
four papers side by side

01:15:12.730 --> 01:15:15.030
is a category of periodization,

01:15:15.030 --> 01:15:17.250
and all of the papers have an attention

01:15:17.250 --> 01:15:20.940
to both the historical narrative

01:15:20.940 --> 01:15:24.300
and the creation of that
historical narrative

01:15:24.300 --> 01:15:26.683
in their perspective topics.

01:15:27.710 --> 01:15:29.730
Adam does this in two ways:

01:15:29.730 --> 01:15:34.340
he shows that entrepreneurship
persisted across 1949

01:15:34.340 --> 01:15:37.210
and that the reform era entrepreneurship

01:15:37.210 --> 01:15:41.110
was not released by the policies
of reform and opening up,

01:15:41.110 --> 01:15:44.420
but was rather preceded by
a wave of entrepreneurship

01:15:44.420 --> 01:15:47.370
that began in the mid 1970s.

01:15:47.370 --> 01:15:50.010
Adam is also able to use data

01:15:50.010 --> 01:15:52.270
to show how activity doesn't just decline

01:15:52.270 --> 01:15:54.570
after so called socialist transformation,

01:15:54.570 --> 01:15:56.200
but that it comes in waves

01:15:56.200 --> 01:15:58.700
despite the interruptions and bureaucracy

01:15:58.700 --> 01:16:00.593
created by the Cultural Revolution.

01:16:01.620 --> 01:16:04.800
Gavin's research invites us
to think about periodization

01:16:04.800 --> 01:16:09.370
by framing perhaps periods of
socialism and high socialism,

01:16:09.370 --> 01:16:12.440
or a long and short Cultural Revolution.

01:16:12.440 --> 01:16:14.860
Thus, the early 1970s for Gavin

01:16:14.860 --> 01:16:16.480
is the beginning of a transformation

01:16:16.480 --> 01:16:17.860
in the service industry,

01:16:17.860 --> 01:16:20.470
one that also maps on to China's admission

01:16:20.470 --> 01:16:21.773
to the United Nations.

01:16:22.960 --> 01:16:26.770
Yanjie's paper periodizes is in two ways:

01:16:26.770 --> 01:16:31.030
on a specific level, he links
the sent-down youth experience

01:16:31.030 --> 01:16:33.440
with youth discontent in the years after,

01:16:33.440 --> 01:16:36.820
so a transition periodization.

01:16:36.820 --> 01:16:38.270
But on a longer time scale,

01:16:38.270 --> 01:16:39.810
and this comes out more so in the paper

01:16:39.810 --> 01:16:41.080
than in the presentation,

01:16:41.080 --> 01:16:44.650
he connects a long history
of what Xiaokang meant

01:16:46.640 --> 01:16:49.360
with Xiao Japing's
reformulation of Xiaokang

01:16:49.360 --> 01:16:50.983
for the era of reform.

01:16:52.010 --> 01:16:55.340
And finally, Peter's study
of management education

01:16:56.430 --> 01:16:57.750
particularly in a paper,

01:16:57.750 --> 01:16:59.350
a close reading of the textbook

01:16:59.350 --> 01:17:01.460
industrial enterprise management

01:17:01.460 --> 01:17:04.870
shows how the history of Mao
era scientific management

01:17:04.870 --> 01:17:07.770
was rewritten for the age of
educating a new generation

01:17:07.770 --> 01:17:10.033
of managers and entrepreneurs.

01:17:11.500 --> 01:17:13.550
Under this larger category
of economic history,

01:17:13.550 --> 01:17:16.500
I want to mention two other
sub categories briefly

01:17:16.500 --> 01:17:18.390
in the interest of time.

01:17:18.390 --> 01:17:20.940
Related to the category
of geographic space,

01:17:20.940 --> 01:17:22.340
there's a theme of connections

01:17:22.340 --> 01:17:24.130
with the world outside of China

01:17:25.750 --> 01:17:29.270
highlighted particularly
in Gavin and Peter's work.

01:17:29.270 --> 01:17:30.870
And secondly, an important theme

01:17:30.870 --> 01:17:32.530
in the study of economic history,

01:17:32.530 --> 01:17:34.040
there's this idea of family

01:17:34.040 --> 01:17:35.730
and intergenerational relationships,

01:17:35.730 --> 01:17:38.620
something that Yenjie's just
paper calls our attention to.

01:17:38.620 --> 01:17:41.360
That is if one aspect of
Chinese business history

01:17:41.360 --> 01:17:43.870
is the role of the corporate family,

01:17:43.870 --> 01:17:45.980
and if Mao era policies have been seen

01:17:45.980 --> 01:17:49.720
as disrupting family networks
or families strategies,

01:17:49.720 --> 01:17:53.210
Yanjie shows us how families
continued to strategize

01:17:53.210 --> 01:17:56.320
as a family to maximize
their economic well being

01:17:56.320 --> 01:17:57.393
in the Mao period.

01:17:58.660 --> 01:18:00.720
The second category or the second theme

01:18:00.720 --> 01:18:02.713
is the importance of ideology.

01:18:03.960 --> 01:18:06.580
I want to underscore how all these papers

01:18:06.580 --> 01:18:09.940
demonstrate the continued
importance of using ideology

01:18:09.940 --> 01:18:14.420
to analyze the experience of
Chinese socialism and after.

01:18:14.420 --> 01:18:17.510
For Adam, ideology is
one of the entry points.

01:18:17.510 --> 01:18:19.520
He can trace the reoccurrences

01:18:19.520 --> 01:18:22.890
of speculation and profiteering
in the People's Daily,

01:18:22.890 --> 01:18:25.770
and then he can show how despite
rhetoric to the contrary,

01:18:25.770 --> 01:18:28.110
local officials did not prosecute cases

01:18:28.110 --> 01:18:30.033
in a way that reflected the rhetoric.

01:18:31.180 --> 01:18:33.810
For Gavin, the ideological
tension of service work

01:18:33.810 --> 01:18:36.090
is something that his historical actors

01:18:36.090 --> 01:18:37.210
have to grapple with.

01:18:37.210 --> 01:18:38.630
And throughout his paper

01:18:38.630 --> 01:18:40.830
and throughout the longer dissertation,

01:18:40.830 --> 01:18:43.760
he traces the political meaning of tourism

01:18:43.760 --> 01:18:45.343
and the tourist industry.

01:18:46.750 --> 01:18:49.690
For Yanjie, the research
question is to understand

01:18:49.690 --> 01:18:52.530
the ways in which popular
sentiments shifted,

01:18:52.530 --> 01:18:57.020
and how ideological slogans
reflected such sentiments.

01:18:57.020 --> 01:18:58.860
And for Peter, in his own words,

01:18:58.860 --> 01:19:01.040
the existence of the MBA programs,

01:19:01.040 --> 01:19:03.360
quote, seemed to pose a paradox

01:19:03.360 --> 01:19:06.230
and the ideological acrobatics
of the management text

01:19:06.230 --> 01:19:09.360
becomes a way to explore
the meaning of management

01:19:09.360 --> 01:19:10.843
in the era of reform.

01:19:12.200 --> 01:19:14.200
And then finally,

01:19:14.200 --> 01:19:16.630
as Yanjie did at the
end of his presentation,

01:19:16.630 --> 01:19:19.920
I wanna highlight the use of new sources

01:19:19.920 --> 01:19:23.430
and applaud the panelists
for their research methods.

01:19:23.430 --> 01:19:27.090
Each in their own way is doing
a form of grassroots history,

01:19:27.090 --> 01:19:29.270
an aggregation of personal experience

01:19:29.270 --> 01:19:32.340
from the Shanghai family,
to the rural entrepreneur,

01:19:32.340 --> 01:19:35.670
to the railway worker, to the MBA student.

01:19:35.670 --> 01:19:38.240
In particular, Adams work
calls for the building

01:19:38.240 --> 01:19:41.410
of micro data sets from grassroots data,

01:19:41.410 --> 01:19:44.460
so I urge you to read the paper
if it's publicly available,

01:19:44.460 --> 01:19:46.640
and also to watch his presentation

01:19:46.640 --> 01:19:48.800
for the Stanford car conference

01:19:48.800 --> 01:19:51.610
on Chinese archives in crisis.

01:19:51.610 --> 01:19:54.160
So data and mapping becomes
increasingly important

01:19:54.160 --> 01:19:55.880
in thinking about new directions

01:19:55.880 --> 01:19:58.670
for contemporary Chinese history.

01:19:58.670 --> 01:20:01.030
And finally, Yanjie's paper

01:20:01.030 --> 01:20:03.190
makes exceptional and pioneering work

01:20:03.190 --> 01:20:06.810
with Mao era personal letters
providing an intimate glimpse

01:20:06.810 --> 01:20:08.930
into both family relationships

01:20:08.930 --> 01:20:11.470
and even a history of family emotions

01:20:11.470 --> 01:20:16.453
and secondly the mentalities
of the reform era experience.

01:20:17.420 --> 01:20:18.920
So in the interest of time,

01:20:18.920 --> 01:20:20.867
I'm only going to ask a couple questions

01:20:20.867 --> 01:20:22.260
out of each panelist

01:20:22.260 --> 01:20:25.213
and I'm sure many more
will come out in the Q&amp;A.

01:20:26.950 --> 01:20:29.710
So, Adam, I already got to
ask you a series of questions

01:20:29.710 --> 01:20:31.420
after the Stanford conference

01:20:31.420 --> 01:20:32.780
so perhaps you can indulge me

01:20:32.780 --> 01:20:35.513
by thinking about a
more technical question.

01:20:36.390 --> 01:20:39.670
So when I was reading your
analysis of the local officials

01:20:39.670 --> 01:20:42.070
on page 25 and 26 of the paper,

01:20:42.070 --> 01:20:43.020
I was thinking about

01:20:43.020 --> 01:20:45.723
things like customs
violations and smuggling.

01:20:46.710 --> 01:20:48.040
So if you read, let's say,

01:20:48.040 --> 01:20:50.500
customs gazetteers from the 1990s,

01:20:50.500 --> 01:20:52.170
their descriptions of what goods

01:20:52.170 --> 01:20:54.350
were still able to pass through customs

01:20:54.350 --> 01:20:57.890
if they were illegal
because they were needed.

01:20:57.890 --> 01:21:00.240
So one case of this is pharmaceuticals.

01:21:00.240 --> 01:21:02.470
So customs officials
would look the other way

01:21:02.470 --> 01:21:05.960
if there was a particular
medicine that was scarce in China.

01:21:05.960 --> 01:21:08.230
And as I was listening
to your presentation,

01:21:08.230 --> 01:21:11.110
I was thinking about some
archival documents I've seen

01:21:11.110 --> 01:21:14.500
where you have purchased
priceless at customs

01:21:14.500 --> 01:21:17.750
with often things like small
electronic goods and so on

01:21:17.750 --> 01:21:19.600
that the customs officials are buying

01:21:21.030 --> 01:21:22.590
from travelers who are bringing in

01:21:22.590 --> 01:21:25.300
more than they're allowed to bring in.

01:21:25.300 --> 01:21:27.960
So in your analysis, is this parallel

01:21:27.960 --> 01:21:30.930
to local officials treatment
of entrepreneurship,

01:21:30.930 --> 01:21:33.840
or are imported goods to be understood

01:21:33.840 --> 01:21:35.430
in a different category?

01:21:35.430 --> 01:21:36.590
In one of your final slides,

01:21:36.590 --> 01:21:39.360
you showed how agriculture
and consumer goods

01:21:39.360 --> 01:21:42.410
were not punished in the
same way as other items.

01:21:42.410 --> 01:21:45.560
So could we look at these
customs imported goods

01:21:45.560 --> 01:21:48.143
in that same vein?

01:21:50.150 --> 01:21:52.363
A second question for Gavin.

01:21:53.380 --> 01:21:57.410
So I just kind of by coincidence,

01:21:57.410 --> 01:22:00.027
I read your paper right
after Peter's paper

01:22:00.027 --> 01:22:02.990
and so I wanted to ask you
more about tourism textbooks

01:22:02.990 --> 01:22:04.650
in tourism education.

01:22:04.650 --> 01:22:06.820
I noticed that in your presentation

01:22:06.820 --> 01:22:08.120
you showed us a photograph

01:22:09.380 --> 01:22:12.460
of a handbook for tourism workers.

01:22:12.460 --> 01:22:15.300
And so thinking through Peter's analysis

01:22:15.300 --> 01:22:16.700
of management education,

01:22:16.700 --> 01:22:18.940
can you tell us more
about tourism education

01:22:18.940 --> 01:22:21.740
and how it changed in your period

01:22:21.740 --> 01:22:24.590
for the presentations today
and perhaps even after?

01:22:24.590 --> 01:22:26.620
When did schools of tourism appear

01:22:26.620 --> 01:22:28.420
and do they have any parallels

01:22:28.420 --> 01:22:32.083
to other forms of education
in the reform era transition?

01:22:33.820 --> 01:22:35.200
For Yanjie, I was hoping

01:22:35.200 --> 01:22:38.010
to ask you a methodological question.

01:22:38.010 --> 01:22:40.000
One of the things you did
really well in the paper

01:22:40.000 --> 01:22:42.910
is to link the very
different kinds of sources.

01:22:42.910 --> 01:22:47.510
On the one hand, you showed us slides

01:22:47.510 --> 01:22:52.000
where you were aggregating
data from labor gazetteers,

01:22:52.000 --> 01:22:54.520
but then when you look
at the family letters,

01:22:54.520 --> 01:22:55.690
especially in your paper,

01:22:55.690 --> 01:22:58.350
these are really about
intimate family relationships

01:22:58.350 --> 01:23:01.700
and emotions and conflicts
within the family.

01:23:01.700 --> 01:23:04.180
So I wanted to ask you about

01:23:04.180 --> 01:23:07.110
what are the methodological
opportunities and challenges

01:23:07.110 --> 01:23:10.223
of dealing with very, very
different kinds of data?

01:23:12.210 --> 01:23:16.430
And then finally, Peter,
you ended your paper

01:23:16.430 --> 01:23:19.000
with a number of questions
that you hope to answer

01:23:19.000 --> 01:23:20.660
in the future.

01:23:20.660 --> 01:23:21.990
To follow up on your conclusion,

01:23:21.990 --> 01:23:24.180
I was thinking of two questions.

01:23:24.180 --> 01:23:26.300
One of them was have
you found out more since

01:23:26.300 --> 01:23:30.810
about the ways in which
textbooks were written?

01:23:30.810 --> 01:23:34.610
My ignorant understanding
of MBA training in the U.S.

01:23:34.610 --> 01:23:36.633
is that it's based on this case method,

01:23:37.610 --> 01:23:39.563
so was this way of teaching

01:23:39.563 --> 01:23:42.260
adapted to the Chinese context and how?

01:23:42.260 --> 01:23:45.920
What kinds of businesses
were used as cases

01:23:45.920 --> 01:23:50.410
when one was studying
in management in China?

01:23:50.410 --> 01:23:51.770
And then as a second question,

01:23:51.770 --> 01:23:54.743
your conclusion got me thinking
about financial journalism.

01:23:55.850 --> 01:23:59.300
So does financial
journalism develop in tandem

01:23:59.300 --> 01:24:01.780
or after management education?

01:24:01.780 --> 01:24:04.170
Do you find similar ties with Hong Kong?

01:24:04.170 --> 01:24:05.930
I'm thinking about economic reporting

01:24:05.930 --> 01:24:08.350
coming out of South China, for example.

01:24:08.350 --> 01:24:10.180
What's the justification and theory

01:24:10.180 --> 01:24:11.950
of financial journalism

01:24:11.950 --> 01:24:14.350
which is often seen as something
that pushes the envelope

01:24:14.350 --> 01:24:17.403
on reporting in contemporary China?

01:24:18.790 --> 01:24:23.780
So perhaps what we can do
is invite each panelist

01:24:23.780 --> 01:24:28.780
to respond to these questions
or the discussant comments,

01:24:30.250 --> 01:24:32.900
and then we'll gather some of the Q&amp;A

01:24:32.900 --> 01:24:35.583
that I'm seeing popping
up in the Q&amp;A function.

01:24:43.630 --> 01:24:46.690
- And thank you, Denise,
for this really generous

01:24:46.690 --> 01:24:48.530
and insightful comments.

01:24:48.530 --> 01:24:51.230
I think we can start with maybe Gavin

01:24:51.230 --> 01:24:53.283
and then we go around in the same order.

01:24:54.762 --> 01:24:56.257
- Thank you, Yanjie,
and thank you, Denise,

01:24:56.257 --> 01:24:57.823
for those comments.

01:24:58.740 --> 01:25:02.020
I think first of all to
answer your specific question

01:25:02.020 --> 01:25:04.590
about the tourism training,

01:25:04.590 --> 01:25:08.070
I had the good fortune
to spend a year in China

01:25:08.070 --> 01:25:10.350
doing fieldwork as sort
of a visiting student

01:25:10.350 --> 01:25:12.930
at one of the tourism schools

01:25:12.930 --> 01:25:15.103
at Sun Yat-sen University in Guangzhou.

01:25:16.449 --> 01:25:19.150
And, of course, what the
students there are studying

01:25:19.150 --> 01:25:23.690
is a much higher level
about sort of the sociology

01:25:23.690 --> 01:25:25.070
and economics of tourism,

01:25:25.070 --> 01:25:27.150
and not sort of so much
the practical training.

01:25:27.150 --> 01:25:28.320
But we definitely see

01:25:28.320 --> 01:25:30.740
in kind of roughly around the time

01:25:30.740 --> 01:25:33.130
in the four modernizations,
the late 1970s,

01:25:33.130 --> 01:25:36.240
we see in Shanghai and
Beijing and Guangzhou,

01:25:36.240 --> 01:25:40.310
the first sort of like practical
tourism training schools

01:25:40.310 --> 01:25:42.160
coming into being,

01:25:42.160 --> 01:25:44.710
and I think the first model,

01:25:44.710 --> 01:25:47.880
and the first model for many
sort of things in China,

01:25:47.880 --> 01:25:50.830
in the new China, is the
sort of the Soviet model.

01:25:50.830 --> 01:25:53.330
And so in the 50s and
even to the early 60s,

01:25:53.330 --> 01:25:54.900
we see much of the learning

01:25:54.900 --> 01:25:57.720
about how to do the business of tourism

01:25:57.720 --> 01:26:00.450
coming from the sort of
the model of interest

01:26:00.450 --> 01:26:03.390
which existed in the Soviet
Union since the 1920s.

01:26:03.390 --> 01:26:06.450
And after the sort of the Soviet model

01:26:06.450 --> 01:26:09.130
is no longer sort of is now revisionist,

01:26:09.130 --> 01:26:10.690
a lot of the training initially,

01:26:10.690 --> 01:26:12.000
a lot of the circulation of knowledge

01:26:12.000 --> 01:26:14.860
is really through direct observation,

01:26:14.860 --> 01:26:16.540
it's sort of seeking truth from facts,

01:26:16.540 --> 01:26:19.100
it's a direct observation
of the tourists themselves.

01:26:19.100 --> 01:26:20.790
And so guides are taking notes

01:26:20.790 --> 01:26:24.470
not just on the sort of
the reactions of tourists

01:26:24.470 --> 01:26:26.390
to sort of like the political
reactions of tourists,

01:26:26.390 --> 01:26:29.250
but also on their reactions to service

01:26:29.250 --> 01:26:32.370
and like trying to learn directly

01:26:32.370 --> 01:26:36.030
from the reactions of foreign tourists.

01:26:36.030 --> 01:26:39.360
And I mean, I think finally
what we see in the 70s and 80s,

01:26:39.360 --> 01:26:40.550
and this, I think, really relates

01:26:40.550 --> 01:26:42.570
to what Peter talked about,

01:26:42.570 --> 01:26:44.500
is we really see the
importance of Hong Kong,

01:26:44.500 --> 01:26:47.690
because Hong Kong was always
sort of the entry point

01:26:47.690 --> 01:26:49.150
for tourists into China.

01:26:49.150 --> 01:26:52.580
I mean, the vast majority of
tourists are coming into China

01:26:52.580 --> 01:26:53.680
from Hong Kong,

01:26:53.680 --> 01:26:54.950
and the vast majority of the tourists

01:26:54.950 --> 01:26:57.730
are residents of Hong Kong.

01:26:57.730 --> 01:27:00.680
And so we're seeing a lot of
learning coming from Hong Kong,

01:27:00.680 --> 01:27:03.110
we're seeing like in the early 80s

01:27:03.110 --> 01:27:06.770
hotels in Shanghai are sending
chefs to Hong Kong hotels

01:27:06.770 --> 01:27:09.770
to train there also.

01:27:09.770 --> 01:27:14.340
The early, particularly
as China's tourism grows,

01:27:14.340 --> 01:27:17.550
the main constraint is the lack of hotels.

01:27:17.550 --> 01:27:19.410
And so the Hong Kong investors

01:27:19.410 --> 01:27:22.530
sort of play a really critical
role in financing hotels,

01:27:22.530 --> 01:27:26.540
and also in designing and
managing those hotels.

01:27:26.540 --> 01:27:27.640
So I mean, that's what I would say

01:27:27.640 --> 01:27:29.700
to that specific question.

01:27:29.700 --> 01:27:33.050
You give me so many other
things that I could talk about,

01:27:33.050 --> 01:27:35.610
I don't wanna take too
much time from the others.

01:27:35.610 --> 01:27:38.080
But one thing I do
wanna want to highlight,

01:27:38.080 --> 01:27:39.610
which I didn't really get a chance

01:27:39.610 --> 01:27:43.280
to sort of highlight so
much in my talk today

01:27:43.280 --> 01:27:44.300
'cause of the time constraints

01:27:44.300 --> 01:27:46.963
was really the role of gender and service,

01:27:48.274 --> 01:27:51.200
and I think that relates to the economics

01:27:51.200 --> 01:27:52.690
of tourism as well.

01:27:52.690 --> 01:27:54.710
I mean, people, particularly men,

01:27:54.710 --> 01:27:56.347
do not want to be service workers,

01:27:56.347 --> 01:27:59.200
that's primarily because
they see it as women's work

01:27:59.200 --> 01:28:02.750
and they say it's a
sector dominated by women

01:28:03.730 --> 01:28:05.470
and they repeated the complain

01:28:05.470 --> 01:28:09.360
about how it sort of damages
their marriage prospects.

01:28:09.360 --> 01:28:11.950
So, and I mean, that goes for the men,

01:28:11.950 --> 01:28:15.810
but even women engaged in service industry

01:28:15.810 --> 01:28:18.940
really see it as a dead end,
it's an unskilled labor.

01:28:18.940 --> 01:28:21.070
So as this, and I think this also adds

01:28:21.070 --> 01:28:22.000
to what Yanjie was talking about,

01:28:22.000 --> 01:28:24.090
as we have more and more sort of youths

01:28:24.090 --> 01:28:27.260
coming back into the urban
workforce in the late 70s,

01:28:27.260 --> 01:28:30.250
we have to sort of funnel more
of them into service trades,

01:28:30.250 --> 01:28:32.950
and that sort of sectors
really doubles in employment

01:28:32.950 --> 01:28:34.980
from 1970 to 1982.

01:28:34.980 --> 01:28:36.210
So there's a lot of new ways

01:28:36.210 --> 01:28:39.137
to sort of try to make it
more acceptable to people,

01:28:39.137 --> 01:28:40.010
and one of the ways

01:28:40.010 --> 01:28:43.710
is through sort of
professionalization of it.

01:28:43.710 --> 01:28:46.000
And I think that sort of that ideology

01:28:46.000 --> 01:28:51.000
of professionalization really
does feed into how we justify,

01:28:53.095 --> 01:28:56.360
how do we reconcile
people's sort of aspirations

01:28:56.360 --> 01:28:59.583
with the reality of the
labor market at that time?

01:29:00.451 --> 01:29:05.451
Thank you.

01:29:06.440 --> 01:29:09.830
- Thank you so much for those
great questions and comments,

01:29:09.830 --> 01:29:13.040
and try do them in the reverse order.

01:29:13.040 --> 01:29:14.810
The financial journalism is something

01:29:14.810 --> 01:29:16.420
I have not thought about at all.

01:29:16.420 --> 01:29:18.223
So that's really interesting.

01:29:19.633 --> 01:29:24.560
As to the intellectual and
potential personnel links there,

01:29:24.560 --> 01:29:26.910
thinking about magazines
or other publications

01:29:26.910 --> 01:29:28.810
like Xai Jing and their origins

01:29:28.810 --> 01:29:31.080
and the kind of knowledge
that you would need to have

01:29:31.080 --> 01:29:34.080
in order to pursue financial journalism,

01:29:34.080 --> 01:29:35.220
but I really don't know

01:29:35.220 --> 01:29:36.810
that much about that aspect of it

01:29:36.810 --> 01:29:40.233
and that's a new avenue to go down.

01:29:41.950 --> 01:29:43.460
The textbooks is really interesting,

01:29:43.460 --> 01:29:46.900
because, and there's a lot going on there,

01:29:46.900 --> 01:29:49.820
because I think that industrial
enterprise management

01:29:49.820 --> 01:29:52.770
was the textbook in the Chinese language

01:29:52.770 --> 01:29:53.880
for a lot of the 1980s.

01:29:53.880 --> 01:29:55.350
It goes through a ton of editions.

01:29:55.350 --> 01:29:58.030
I haven't gotten my hands
on all of the additions

01:29:58.030 --> 01:30:01.650
and it seems to be a prestigious thing

01:30:01.650 --> 01:30:04.850
to be appointed to the council
that's writing the textbook,

01:30:04.850 --> 01:30:08.240
and they're selecting a
kind of expanding number

01:30:08.240 --> 01:30:13.240
of co-authors to revise the
various editions of this text.

01:30:13.400 --> 01:30:15.800
But then when we get to the 90s

01:30:15.800 --> 01:30:20.800
and the partnership and the
transition towards MBA programs,

01:30:21.600 --> 01:30:24.060
there's this interesting
kind of bifurcation

01:30:24.060 --> 01:30:28.050
because in particular the
programs at Fudan and Tsinghua

01:30:28.050 --> 01:30:30.860
pride themselves on being in English,

01:30:30.860 --> 01:30:33.170
and the textbooks they continue to use

01:30:33.170 --> 01:30:36.750
are primarily the exact
same textbook as MIT

01:30:36.750 --> 01:30:39.790
or other programs,

01:30:39.790 --> 01:30:42.780
the managerial communications
textbook being one example

01:30:42.780 --> 01:30:45.150
or "Data, Models, and Decisions".

01:30:45.150 --> 01:30:46.550
But "Data, Models, and Decisions",

01:30:46.550 --> 01:30:48.370
which I didn't go into too far,

01:30:48.370 --> 01:30:51.080
has a number of translations

01:30:51.080 --> 01:30:52.700
some which seem to be authorized,

01:30:52.700 --> 01:30:54.600
and some of which seem to be unauthorized.

01:30:54.600 --> 01:30:56.730
And the whole point of
data models and decisions

01:30:56.730 --> 01:30:59.120
is to work through specific cases.

01:30:59.120 --> 01:31:02.120
So the involved faculty have talked about

01:31:02.120 --> 01:31:03.560
in the authorized translations,

01:31:03.560 --> 01:31:06.830
they have begun to
choose new Chinese cases

01:31:06.830 --> 01:31:08.240
that are more closely tailored

01:31:08.240 --> 01:31:09.890
to what students will anticipate

01:31:09.890 --> 01:31:11.533
and experience in the future,

01:31:12.870 --> 01:31:15.900
but oftentimes, the translation
is just an exact translation

01:31:15.900 --> 01:31:18.960
of the English version.

01:31:18.960 --> 01:31:21.073
So there's similarly more
work that I can do there,

01:31:21.073 --> 01:31:23.470
some which goes out of my timeframe,

01:31:23.470 --> 01:31:25.920
this is the last chapter of the project

01:31:25.920 --> 01:31:27.320
so we'll see where I end up.

01:31:28.630 --> 01:31:30.560
But thank you, both of
those are great avenues

01:31:30.560 --> 01:31:32.903
to continue researching on.

01:31:41.730 --> 01:31:42.653
- So maybe Adam?

01:31:44.940 --> 01:31:46.500
- Sorry.

01:31:46.500 --> 01:31:49.010
Yeah, no, that's an interesting question

01:31:49.010 --> 01:31:52.970
about the goods that are
being smuggled through

01:31:52.970 --> 01:31:54.740
and then like the
permissibility of those goods,

01:31:54.740 --> 01:31:58.050
I think we can treat
them the exact same way

01:31:58.050 --> 01:31:59.840
as the other types of
goods that I'm looking at.

01:31:59.840 --> 01:32:02.820
So one thing I'm doing
with my data set right now

01:32:02.820 --> 01:32:04.730
is that I've taken all of the goods

01:32:04.730 --> 01:32:06.400
that have been seized by officials,

01:32:06.400 --> 01:32:10.170
and I'm organizing them into
the classification systems

01:32:10.170 --> 01:32:12.140
that were created by government officials,

01:32:12.140 --> 01:32:16.040
so class one, class two,
and then class three goods.

01:32:16.040 --> 01:32:17.220
And then in that we also have

01:32:17.220 --> 01:32:22.220
which administrative departments
oversee the management

01:32:22.300 --> 01:32:23.900
of particular types of goods,

01:32:23.900 --> 01:32:26.200
like the grain department's over grain,

01:32:26.200 --> 01:32:27.110
and then cotton's control,

01:32:27.110 --> 01:32:28.673
but anyway so on so forth.

01:32:29.900 --> 01:32:32.750
And I'm looking at how these
different types of goods

01:32:32.750 --> 01:32:37.620
are variably controlled in the economy,

01:32:37.620 --> 01:32:40.190
I think we can do the exact same thing

01:32:40.190 --> 01:32:41.733
with the custom networks.

01:32:42.720 --> 01:32:43.750
Yeah, I'll stop there.

01:32:43.750 --> 01:32:45.180
I wanna answer some of the questions

01:32:45.180 --> 01:32:47.953
that people have brought up
so I'll leave time for that.

01:32:52.630 --> 01:32:55.080
- Thank you, Denise, for
this wonderful question.

01:32:56.220 --> 01:32:57.680
It's actually a very challenging one

01:32:57.680 --> 01:33:02.220
so I will seek to provide
my very humble opinion

01:33:02.220 --> 01:33:03.540
on this methodology question.

01:33:03.540 --> 01:33:07.393
I think with the current
political situations,

01:33:08.410 --> 01:33:09.580
it become increasingly difficult

01:33:09.580 --> 01:33:12.940
really to secure a complete set of sources

01:33:12.940 --> 01:33:16.310
that goes all the way from provincial

01:33:16.310 --> 01:33:20.010
or even central level archives
to grassroots sources.

01:33:20.010 --> 01:33:23.350
And increasingly, we need to
rely on grassroots sources

01:33:23.350 --> 01:33:26.900
that we have no direct control actually.

01:33:26.900 --> 01:33:31.103
So here basically work on
what we can stop sometimes.

01:33:33.563 --> 01:33:35.530
So there's definitely
some kind of challenges.

01:33:35.530 --> 01:33:39.370
So my approach to this question is that

01:33:39.370 --> 01:33:43.950
first is we need to look
at grassroot sources

01:33:43.950 --> 01:33:48.950
as a new type of challenge
to the historic profession

01:33:50.480 --> 01:33:55.480
because it raises new ethical
questions about the use.

01:33:58.166 --> 01:34:01.640
So the most urgency are actually
for grassroots historians,

01:34:01.640 --> 01:34:03.257
especially for people like me

01:34:03.257 --> 01:34:05.050
who are working private sources

01:34:05.050 --> 01:34:09.660
is that what kind of protocols,

01:34:09.660 --> 01:34:12.870
privacy protocols that we need to adopt

01:34:12.870 --> 01:34:15.750
in order to protect privacy of the,

01:34:15.750 --> 01:34:18.680
I'll say the letter writers or the diarist

01:34:18.680 --> 01:34:23.680
or some people will have some
petitioners to the work units.

01:34:25.190 --> 01:34:26.190
The second question

01:34:27.150 --> 01:34:29.420
is the basic or immediate
challenge, of course,

01:34:29.420 --> 01:34:33.150
sources that is often
incomplete, scattered,

01:34:33.150 --> 01:34:36.740
and sometimes extremely idiosyncratic.

01:34:36.740 --> 01:34:38.240
I think Adam did a fabulous job

01:34:38.240 --> 01:34:42.290
of compiling almost a whole
data set from two companies,

01:34:42.290 --> 01:34:45.990
but that's very rare
among grassroots works

01:34:45.990 --> 01:34:48.370
because most people don't have this,

01:34:48.370 --> 01:34:51.790
sometimes you just don't have
this complete collections.

01:34:51.790 --> 01:34:55.310
So the question is how to
work with more scattered,

01:34:55.310 --> 01:34:57.163
sort of more scattered nature,

01:34:58.290 --> 01:35:03.290
and my experience is that
we need to keep in mind

01:35:07.117 --> 01:35:08.660
the question of scale

01:35:08.660 --> 01:35:11.780
and the question of
information bureaucracy,

01:35:11.780 --> 01:35:15.180
that is how the kind of informations

01:35:15.180 --> 01:35:17.050
we gathered from grassroot sources

01:35:17.050 --> 01:35:19.670
reflect only certain issues,

01:35:19.670 --> 01:35:23.900
or certain aspects of certain
issues at a certain locale.

01:35:23.900 --> 01:35:25.880
So we need to contextualize
it really carefully

01:35:25.880 --> 01:35:29.230
with sources from
archives or other sources,

01:35:29.230 --> 01:35:31.380
or macro data.

01:35:31.380 --> 01:35:32.630
And then we need to bear in mind

01:35:32.630 --> 01:35:34.610
that it's really a very small piece

01:35:34.610 --> 01:35:36.430
of the whole information system,

01:35:36.430 --> 01:35:40.260
that is like churning out
millions of documents,

01:35:40.260 --> 01:35:42.110
private and public, every day.

01:35:42.110 --> 01:35:45.030
So basically, we need to like
piece up the jigsaw puzzle

01:35:45.030 --> 01:35:48.180
to know where are the missing puzzles,

01:35:48.180 --> 01:35:51.070
and where our information
will actually contribute

01:35:51.070 --> 01:35:55.250
to the kind of larger information network

01:35:55.250 --> 01:35:58.853
that was operating in Mao's China.

01:35:59.690 --> 01:36:02.400
So that's my answer

01:36:02.400 --> 01:36:06.670
and I'll keep on thinking
about your question later.

01:36:06.670 --> 01:36:07.503
Thank you.

01:36:15.900 --> 01:36:17.340
I see we had a lot of questions.

01:36:17.340 --> 01:36:21.320
- We have a lot of questions
and about 20 minutes remaining.

01:36:21.320 --> 01:36:26.020
So maybe what we can do
is have a first tranche

01:36:26.020 --> 01:36:28.400
of two questions per speaker

01:36:28.400 --> 01:36:30.100
that you can answer as you wish

01:36:32.953 --> 01:36:35.490
and then we can go to a
second round of questions

01:36:35.490 --> 01:36:36.703
if we have time.

01:36:37.970 --> 01:36:39.963
So let's see, Peter,

01:36:41.754 --> 01:36:46.754
I'm mostly choosing in the
order in which they came in.

01:36:48.170 --> 01:36:52.210
You have a question from,
scrolling through here,

01:36:52.210 --> 01:36:55.553
you have a question from Coji
Hirata and Isabella Jackson.

01:36:58.590 --> 01:37:01.690
Isabella asks, can you please tell us more

01:37:01.690 --> 01:37:05.733
about the students in these MBA programs?

01:37:06.690 --> 01:37:11.690
And Coji asks, I assume
many of the students

01:37:12.090 --> 01:37:14.680
in management education
were employed by SOEs

01:37:14.680 --> 01:37:16.750
rather than private enterprises,

01:37:16.750 --> 01:37:19.960
did the still dominant
presence of SOEs in China

01:37:19.960 --> 01:37:24.400
influence the content of MBA
education in a substantial way?

01:37:24.400 --> 01:37:28.050
Also, were these programs related
to Zhu Rongji's SOU reform

01:37:28.050 --> 01:37:29.610
in any way?

01:37:29.610 --> 01:37:32.203
So let's do two questions for Peter.

01:37:33.970 --> 01:37:35.790
Two questions for you Yanjie,

01:37:35.790 --> 01:37:38.693
we have one from Sarah Chang.

01:37:41.543 --> 01:37:44.060
The question has to do with
state-family relations.

01:37:44.060 --> 01:37:45.420
Scholars in the socialist period

01:37:45.420 --> 01:37:47.330
sometimes characterize the connection

01:37:47.330 --> 01:37:49.980
between state and family
as a mutual embeddedness

01:37:49.980 --> 01:37:51.500
where the family was a smaller cell

01:37:51.500 --> 01:37:53.420
within a larger production regime,

01:37:53.420 --> 01:37:56.060
would you say this relation
is radically different

01:37:56.060 --> 01:37:57.700
in a Xiaokang society?

01:37:57.700 --> 01:38:00.150
Does the family break out of
this relation with the state?

01:38:00.150 --> 01:38:02.650
What bearing does this
have on contemporary China?

01:38:04.430 --> 01:38:07.717
And a second question from Coji,

01:38:13.710 --> 01:38:18.010
Coji Hirata, is the youngest
vision of Xiaokang society

01:38:18.010 --> 01:38:21.433
related to the one
child policy in any way?

01:38:24.000 --> 01:38:27.940
Then let's see two questions for Adam.

01:38:27.940 --> 01:38:31.330
I don't know if Adam you
would prefer to choose them,

01:38:31.330 --> 01:38:33.230
or go with the first two that came in?

01:38:36.345 --> 01:38:38.110
- Either is fine.

01:38:38.110 --> 01:38:40.820
- Okay, well, if we go with
the first two that came in,

01:38:40.820 --> 01:38:42.193
we have one from Huanjajo.

01:38:46.150 --> 01:38:48.270
I wonder if you tried
to look for these people

01:38:48.270 --> 01:38:50.310
engaged in such activities
or their relatives

01:38:50.310 --> 01:38:52.450
to learn more about how being caught

01:38:53.640 --> 01:38:56.160
in the practice of illicit
entrepreneurial activities

01:38:56.160 --> 01:38:58.170
may have influenced their life,

01:38:58.170 --> 01:38:59.910
was there any non-formal punishment

01:38:59.910 --> 01:39:01.713
such as a loss of reputation?

01:39:02.900 --> 01:39:05.700
And you also have an early
question from Aaron Glassman.

01:39:06.842 --> 01:39:08.720
Is there a difference
between a local official

01:39:08.720 --> 01:39:12.360
who corruptly profits on local
violation of socialist policy

01:39:12.360 --> 01:39:16.273
and a local official who
fosters entrepreneurship?

01:39:18.320 --> 01:39:21.760
And there's a longer question
but the wrap up question is,

01:39:21.760 --> 01:39:24.270
why isn't this just a story
about local corruption?

01:39:24.270 --> 01:39:26.080
What does your research have to say

01:39:26.080 --> 01:39:28.180
about rule of law in the PRC?

01:39:30.919 --> 01:39:34.836
And I don't have an
audience question for Gavin

01:39:36.880 --> 01:39:39.230
so maybe I'll take the chairs prerogative

01:39:39.230 --> 01:39:42.240
and ask you about the role
of the China Travel Service

01:39:42.240 --> 01:39:44.690
which comes up in your dissertation

01:39:44.690 --> 01:39:47.240
and you're able to discuss at length.

01:39:47.240 --> 01:39:50.340
So I think that for many of us
who study or travel in China,

01:39:50.340 --> 01:39:53.260
we encounter the China Travel Service

01:39:53.260 --> 01:39:55.210
in our own going around.

01:39:55.210 --> 01:39:56.970
And so I wondered if you
would talk more about

01:39:56.970 --> 01:39:57.977
the history of that

01:39:59.330 --> 01:40:01.620
as a private and bureaucratic institution,

01:40:01.620 --> 01:40:04.820
and then when does the emergence

01:40:04.820 --> 01:40:09.820
of private or local
travel agencies appear?

01:40:18.539 --> 01:40:19.372
Shall we?

01:40:19.372 --> 01:40:20.800
Sure, we'll start with Peter.

01:40:23.260 --> 01:40:24.410
- Sorry, I didn't know.

01:40:26.490 --> 01:40:28.930
And thank you to the audience
members for these questions

01:40:28.930 --> 01:40:30.700
that kind of overlap

01:40:30.700 --> 01:40:33.550
so I think I'll just kind of
take them together, I think,

01:40:34.510 --> 01:40:35.950
in terms of the students

01:40:35.950 --> 01:40:40.553
in that there's this really
interesting evolution

01:40:42.220 --> 01:40:46.820
in the nature and origin of
the students in these programs,

01:40:46.820 --> 01:40:51.620
and that as the questions
implied that in the 1980s,

01:40:51.620 --> 01:40:54.010
you really aren't talking about primarily

01:40:54.010 --> 01:40:56.700
state-owned enterprise and party managers

01:40:56.700 --> 01:41:01.700
of various divisions and
pieces of the bureaucracy

01:41:02.690 --> 01:41:03.870
and state industry,

01:41:03.870 --> 01:41:07.360
many of whom are being assigned be pei fu

01:41:07.360 --> 01:41:08.810
to these programs,

01:41:08.810 --> 01:41:11.100
for sometimes it's just two weeks

01:41:11.100 --> 01:41:15.120
for what we would probably
call an extended conference

01:41:15.120 --> 01:41:17.450
or something along those lines

01:41:17.450 --> 01:41:20.070
to wholesale one years or two years

01:41:20.070 --> 01:41:23.453
of re-education of various kinds.

01:41:25.520 --> 01:41:26.660
What's interesting there, though,

01:41:26.660 --> 01:41:29.800
is there does not seem
to be a particular link

01:41:29.800 --> 01:41:33.360
between who the students were in the 1980s

01:41:33.360 --> 01:41:36.350
and the nature of the content
that they're being given.

01:41:36.350 --> 01:41:41.010
And that I do not have as
many syllabi and curriculum

01:41:41.010 --> 01:41:42.383
such as I would like,

01:41:43.900 --> 01:41:47.580
but that a lot of the
time what is being taught

01:41:47.580 --> 01:41:52.260
seems to be determined more by
the various forms of partners

01:41:52.260 --> 01:41:54.810
and partnerships that are
structuring these programs.

01:41:54.810 --> 01:41:59.330
And so a good example is
that the Yijibu program

01:41:59.330 --> 01:42:03.250
be it with Battat mostly
in the the commanding role

01:42:03.250 --> 01:42:07.283
is largely copying MIT's MBA
program from the beginning.

01:42:08.130 --> 01:42:10.110
The Shanghai Municipal Program,

01:42:10.110 --> 01:42:14.320
which the students are
very much being assigned

01:42:14.320 --> 01:42:19.320
from city enterprises in our
mid level career officials,

01:42:19.690 --> 01:42:20.870
is being, in part, run

01:42:20.870 --> 01:42:22.740
by the Chinese University of Hong Kong

01:42:22.740 --> 01:42:24.380
by a person who was actually involved

01:42:24.380 --> 01:42:26.660
in the China Travel Service before 1949.

01:42:26.660 --> 01:42:28.300
So that's an interesting link,

01:42:28.300 --> 01:42:30.483
which we can talk about, Kevin,

01:42:31.440 --> 01:42:32.940
and the Beijing Municipal Program

01:42:32.940 --> 01:42:37.400
is being, in part, run by a
Swedish management institute.

01:42:37.400 --> 01:42:38.530
So in the 80s, at least,

01:42:38.530 --> 01:42:40.270
there seems to be this whole smorgasbord

01:42:40.270 --> 01:42:42.670
of different things going on

01:42:42.670 --> 01:42:45.973
that are not necessarily correlated there.

01:42:47.520 --> 01:42:50.320
For the MBA students,
that Isabella's question,

01:42:50.320 --> 01:42:52.630
assuming we're really
talking about the 90s,

01:42:52.630 --> 01:42:57.127
I don't have a lot of firm data about 1991

01:42:58.130 --> 01:42:59.760
when the programs open

01:42:59.760 --> 01:43:02.860
to 1997 when the MIT partnership starts,

01:43:02.860 --> 01:43:06.020
we have a ton of data for after 1997

01:43:06.020 --> 01:43:07.910
because in large part

01:43:07.910 --> 01:43:11.450
MIT strongly encourages Fudan and Tsinghua

01:43:11.450 --> 01:43:13.550
to launch career development centers,

01:43:13.550 --> 01:43:16.510
and those career development
centers keep track

01:43:16.510 --> 01:43:19.960
of the students, who they
are, and what they go on to.

01:43:19.960 --> 01:43:23.083
And as Denise will know from the paper,

01:43:23.970 --> 01:43:25.510
part of what I think is
just really interesting

01:43:25.510 --> 01:43:27.530
is that one of the
things they kept track of

01:43:27.530 --> 01:43:30.810
is what the students made
before, not in terms of money,

01:43:30.810 --> 01:43:31.930
before they entered the program

01:43:31.930 --> 01:43:33.810
and what they make after the program

01:43:33.810 --> 01:43:36.570
and that they experienced huge pay rises

01:43:36.570 --> 01:43:38.640
as the MBA students graduate

01:43:38.640 --> 01:43:41.490
and largely join
multinational corporations

01:43:41.490 --> 01:43:43.530
and foreign joint ventures in China.

01:43:43.530 --> 01:43:47.850
So with the foreign
structured MBA programs,

01:43:47.850 --> 01:43:51.223
you see this interesting
demographic of people

01:43:53.340 --> 01:43:55.870
who are aspiring, it seems,

01:43:55.870 --> 01:43:58.620
or being scaffolded towards careers,

01:43:58.620 --> 01:44:00.520
very different careers in multinationals

01:44:00.520 --> 01:44:03.540
and joint ventures after these programs,

01:44:03.540 --> 01:44:07.163
very different from the
1980s of people being pei fud

01:44:08.090 --> 01:44:09.133
into these programs.

01:44:13.330 --> 01:44:14.540
- I'm I next?

01:44:14.540 --> 01:44:15.400
- Sure.

01:44:15.400 --> 01:44:16.970
- Okay, great.

01:44:16.970 --> 01:44:18.953
Yeah, so just to answer the question,

01:44:20.310 --> 01:44:23.850
so I've interviewed dozens of
former government officials

01:44:23.850 --> 01:44:27.470
who were in charge of prosecuting
illicit entrepreneurship,

01:44:27.470 --> 01:44:31.160
and I've interviewed many
illicit entrepreneurs

01:44:31.160 --> 01:44:33.680
who are engaged in these
underground activities

01:44:33.680 --> 01:44:34.513
in these periods.

01:44:34.513 --> 01:44:35.700
So through oral histories,

01:44:35.700 --> 01:44:40.315
I'm trying to explore
more of those dynamics.

01:44:40.315 --> 01:44:43.030
I was really fortunate to
interview for example, Xia Gonga,

01:44:43.030 --> 01:44:44.359
who was the guy who started that the

01:44:44.359 --> 01:44:46.840
(speaks in foreign language)

01:44:46.840 --> 01:44:48.114
like the commodities market,

01:44:48.114 --> 01:44:52.320
and he's like maybe the
first official in China

01:44:52.320 --> 01:44:57.040
to really allow long
distance trade and commerce

01:44:57.040 --> 01:45:01.050
at the local level prior
to reform and opening up.

01:45:01.050 --> 01:45:03.470
So those kinds of interviews
are really get to explore

01:45:03.470 --> 01:45:06.190
those dynamics a lot more.

01:45:06.190 --> 01:45:09.710
So is this just a story of corruption?

01:45:09.710 --> 01:45:10.623
I don't think so.

01:45:11.620 --> 01:45:14.750
For one, what's particular
about my data set,

01:45:14.750 --> 01:45:17.510
these are the cases of entrepreneurs

01:45:17.510 --> 01:45:19.940
who are prosecuted by the state.

01:45:19.940 --> 01:45:24.240
So in some way like all I
have is examples of failure.

01:45:24.240 --> 01:45:25.650
These are the guys who got caught.

01:45:25.650 --> 01:45:26.483
These are the guys

01:45:26.483 --> 01:45:28.850
who did not successfully
bribe the officials.

01:45:28.850 --> 01:45:32.080
These are the people that show
up in the historical record,

01:45:32.080 --> 01:45:34.230
any really successful listed entrepreneur

01:45:34.230 --> 01:45:36.670
is not part of written history, right?

01:45:36.670 --> 01:45:38.630
I mean, they're just not going to be

01:45:38.630 --> 01:45:40.780
in the documents produced by the state

01:45:40.780 --> 01:45:42.790
prosecuting these individuals.

01:45:42.790 --> 01:45:45.930
And furthermore, if corruption
was the leading story,

01:45:45.930 --> 01:45:48.390
we wouldn't necessarily expect to find

01:45:48.390 --> 01:45:51.660
strongly pattern activity.

01:45:51.660 --> 01:45:54.660
So we would expect that
the bribery would be

01:45:54.660 --> 01:45:56.967
idiosyncratic to the individual,
to the relationships,

01:45:56.967 --> 01:45:58.060
and things like that,

01:45:58.060 --> 01:46:01.340
what I find in my data
sets is highly calculated

01:46:01.340 --> 01:46:05.500
and patterned forms of prosecution.

01:46:05.500 --> 01:46:06.710
You see that officials

01:46:06.710 --> 01:46:09.200
irrespective of who is being prosecuted,

01:46:09.200 --> 01:46:13.110
prosecute some types of
activities a lot more than others,

01:46:13.110 --> 01:46:15.130
and so that's a really important story

01:46:15.130 --> 01:46:16.470
that I'm trying to tell.

01:46:16.470 --> 01:46:21.340
And I got several questions,

01:46:21.340 --> 01:46:22.650
a lot of them deal with

01:46:22.650 --> 01:46:25.920
what was the nature of entrepreneurship?

01:46:25.920 --> 01:46:26.753
How big was it?

01:46:26.753 --> 01:46:29.900
How long did it carry on for?

01:46:29.900 --> 01:46:32.890
So in my dissertation project,

01:46:32.890 --> 01:46:36.410
I look at the persistence
of entrepreneurial activity

01:46:36.410 --> 01:46:40.830
from the very start of like
the socialist high tide,

01:46:40.830 --> 01:46:42.380
all the way to reform and opening up.

01:46:42.380 --> 01:46:45.000
And what I find is these
practices are ubiquitous,

01:46:45.000 --> 01:46:47.460
they're not contained to
particular regions of China

01:46:47.460 --> 01:46:49.650
and they persist throughout
the whole period.

01:46:49.650 --> 01:46:50.860
So right in the Great Leap Forward,

01:46:50.860 --> 01:46:53.560
there is tons of listed
entrepreneurial activity

01:46:53.560 --> 01:46:57.365
which I'm dealing with in the
chapter I'm writing right now.

01:46:57.365 --> 01:47:00.000
And someone asked me
what am I doing different

01:47:00.000 --> 01:47:02.110
than the existing accounts
of entrepreneurship

01:47:02.110 --> 01:47:02.943
on the master?

01:47:02.943 --> 01:47:04.980
There are existing accounts
of entrepreneurship

01:47:04.980 --> 01:47:06.140
in this period,

01:47:06.140 --> 01:47:07.250
but I would say a couple things,

01:47:07.250 --> 01:47:08.570
one, the existing accounts

01:47:08.570 --> 01:47:11.480
really focus on the socialist
transformation period,

01:47:11.480 --> 01:47:13.320
mostly, the really good stuff.

01:47:13.320 --> 01:47:16.090
Robert Kleiber has some
amazing work on this,

01:47:16.090 --> 01:47:17.810
a number of individuals as well,

01:47:17.810 --> 01:47:20.193
but a lot of it really stops at 1958.

01:47:21.080 --> 01:47:23.210
After that, we do get some accounts,

01:47:23.210 --> 01:47:24.910
but they're really heavily biased

01:47:24.910 --> 01:47:26.880
toward Shanghai and Wenzhou,

01:47:26.880 --> 01:47:29.020
like the experiences of
those particular place,

01:47:29.020 --> 01:47:30.590
so they're very idiosyncratic

01:47:30.590 --> 01:47:32.700
and they really don't tell us much

01:47:32.700 --> 01:47:37.440
about the overall picture for China,

01:47:37.440 --> 01:47:40.950
the relationship between
entrepreneurs and the state.

01:47:40.950 --> 01:47:42.440
So that's what I'm
trying to do differently.

01:47:42.440 --> 01:47:44.760
I am building these micro data sets

01:47:44.760 --> 01:47:47.870
to give us a sense of like
the full scale of this.

01:47:47.870 --> 01:47:50.080
So in the paper that I just presented,

01:47:50.080 --> 01:47:53.440
I did 2500 cases that
were from two counties.

01:47:53.440 --> 01:47:54.470
Why those two counties?

01:47:54.470 --> 01:47:56.360
Just simply because I was lucky enough

01:47:56.360 --> 01:47:58.770
to come across really complete collections

01:47:58.770 --> 01:48:00.500
of documents for those two counties.

01:48:00.500 --> 01:48:04.960
But in total, I have more than
8000 cases from across China

01:48:04.960 --> 01:48:06.610
which I'm building into the data set,

01:48:06.610 --> 01:48:08.110
but I have to figure out
how to deal with that data

01:48:08.110 --> 01:48:10.410
because we have all different
kinds, it's incomplete,

01:48:10.410 --> 01:48:13.940
so we have to figure out how
to deal with potential biases

01:48:13.940 --> 01:48:14.870
and things like that.

01:48:14.870 --> 01:48:16.110
So I'm working on that.

01:48:16.110 --> 01:48:17.750
But I think we can also use

01:48:17.750 --> 01:48:19.970
the type of data that I'm
creating in novel ways

01:48:19.970 --> 01:48:23.650
to really get at other
measures of how ubiquitous

01:48:23.650 --> 01:48:26.920
and systemic elicit entrepreneurship is.

01:48:26.920 --> 01:48:29.420
For example, one thing
I'm working on right now

01:48:30.370 --> 01:48:34.020
is I'm using price differentials

01:48:34.020 --> 01:48:36.550
as a measure of the perceived risk

01:48:36.550 --> 01:48:38.810
of illicit entrepreneurial activity.

01:48:38.810 --> 01:48:42.630
So the best kind of way I can think of

01:48:42.630 --> 01:48:45.010
to figure out how systemic,

01:48:45.010 --> 01:48:47.250
as I said, again, my
datasets cases of failure.

01:48:47.250 --> 01:48:51.810
So what size of all cases possible cases

01:48:51.810 --> 01:48:52.643
did those represent?

01:48:52.643 --> 01:48:54.880
That's a really, really
tough question to get at.

01:48:54.880 --> 01:48:57.520
One way that I think I'm
gonna try to get at it,

01:48:57.520 --> 01:49:00.720
is to use the reported price differentials

01:49:00.720 --> 01:49:04.680
in the price that elicit
good like ration coupons,

01:49:04.680 --> 01:49:06.920
cloth rations, grain rations,

01:49:06.920 --> 01:49:10.420
how much they sold for and how
much they were purchased for.

01:49:10.420 --> 01:49:11.660
Now, theoretically,

01:49:11.660 --> 01:49:13.900
at least this is what's going
through my mind right now,

01:49:13.900 --> 01:49:16.290
if we look at the differential in that gap

01:49:16.290 --> 01:49:18.173
between the purchase and sale price,

01:49:19.120 --> 01:49:22.350
entrepreneurs theoretically
would be calculating in

01:49:22.350 --> 01:49:24.530
the potential risk of seizure.

01:49:24.530 --> 01:49:26.800
I mean, these goods have like
zero transportation costs,

01:49:26.800 --> 01:49:29.150
they're super immutable,
it's almost like cash.

01:49:29.150 --> 01:49:30.790
So like the main thing
that would be going through

01:49:30.790 --> 01:49:32.830
entrepreneurs minds when
they're buying and selling

01:49:32.830 --> 01:49:34.430
in terms of profit calculations

01:49:34.430 --> 01:49:36.840
is how likely am I to get caught?

01:49:36.840 --> 01:49:39.290
And what I have found so far

01:49:39.290 --> 01:49:42.930
in the analysis of all these
things is that spread is tiny.

01:49:42.930 --> 01:49:45.850
They were, for example,
selling cloth rations

01:49:45.850 --> 01:49:50.800
for maybe 50 cents a yard,

01:49:50.800 --> 01:49:54.030
and then they were buying
them for 48 cents a yard.

01:49:54.030 --> 01:49:56.160
It's a tiny differential

01:49:56.160 --> 01:49:59.670
which implies that their
expectations of getting caught

01:49:59.670 --> 01:50:02.640
and getting all of those things
ceased is relatively low.

01:50:02.640 --> 01:50:04.450
And then there's other
cost too and things,

01:50:04.450 --> 01:50:08.330
but if we attribute that
spread only to risk,

01:50:08.330 --> 01:50:10.640
we can still get a sense
that this illicit economy

01:50:10.640 --> 01:50:12.990
and from that we can
then estimate backwards

01:50:12.990 --> 01:50:15.920
maybe how many people were
operating in this space.

01:50:15.920 --> 01:50:18.290
So that's the kind of gist of
the thing I'm trying to do,

01:50:18.290 --> 01:50:22.810
provide a very quantitative,
robust, rigorous way

01:50:22.810 --> 01:50:26.380
of getting at the totality
of entrepreneurial activity

01:50:26.380 --> 01:50:27.213
in the economy.

01:50:27.213 --> 01:50:29.680
And then, of course, in
addition to deep readings,

01:50:29.680 --> 01:50:32.340
individual case studies, oral history,

01:50:32.340 --> 01:50:36.230
the stuff that is much more robust

01:50:36.230 --> 01:50:37.980
in the current existing literature.

01:50:43.340 --> 01:50:47.530
- Okay, so great questions.

01:50:47.530 --> 01:50:50.700
So the first question
about these embarkness

01:50:50.700 --> 01:50:52.620
of Chinese family state

01:50:52.620 --> 01:50:56.980
I think you got a point

01:50:56.980 --> 01:51:00.560
about a very crucial issue of foundation,

01:51:00.560 --> 01:51:02.650
I will argue a fundamental issue

01:51:02.650 --> 01:51:06.560
about the Chinese conception
of the state which are right,

01:51:06.560 --> 01:51:08.240
and it's state family.

01:51:08.240 --> 01:51:10.730
So according to the Confucian tradition,

01:51:10.730 --> 01:51:12.700
and especially according to
the traditional Nietzsche,

01:51:12.700 --> 01:51:16.283
where the concept of
Xiaokang really came about,

01:51:18.040 --> 01:51:22.420
family and state were
continuous and inter embodied.

01:51:22.420 --> 01:51:26.450
So first, you started with
the family as foundation

01:51:26.450 --> 01:51:27.763
like a pre-order.

01:51:28.830 --> 01:51:31.700
And there's no like big boundaries

01:51:31.700 --> 01:51:33.190
between what is conceptually family

01:51:33.190 --> 01:51:35.560
and what is conceptually distant

01:51:35.560 --> 01:51:37.153
like in Aristotle's politic.

01:51:39.450 --> 01:51:42.490
So what happens after the Mao's revolution

01:51:42.490 --> 01:51:45.520
was, in fact, I argued in my dissertation,

01:51:45.520 --> 01:51:48.000
a sort of a return to this
conventionalized ideal

01:51:48.000 --> 01:51:49.380
of the family state,

01:51:49.380 --> 01:51:51.950
rather than what happened
during the Great Leap Forward

01:51:51.950 --> 01:51:54.730
which I termed as a state family,

01:51:54.730 --> 01:51:57.270
that is to reduce family to the dependence

01:51:57.270 --> 01:52:01.000
or eventually eliminate the family, right?

01:52:01.000 --> 01:52:02.410
So the individuals who belong

01:52:02.410 --> 01:52:04.630
to this biggest communist society.

01:52:04.630 --> 01:52:06.910
So basically what happened

01:52:06.910 --> 01:52:08.930
after the Mao's revolution, interestingly,

01:52:08.930 --> 01:52:12.330
is a return to more of Confucianism norm,

01:52:12.330 --> 01:52:15.400
and that never happened in Eastern Europe.

01:52:15.400 --> 01:52:20.230
And that's a very big reason
why China actually survived

01:52:20.230 --> 01:52:21.980
the Jun force, right?

01:52:21.980 --> 01:52:24.880
Is because of this fundamental
ideologies of Xiaokang

01:52:24.880 --> 01:52:28.890
that has tied in with
China's old traditions

01:52:28.890 --> 01:52:31.913
of more commercialized
embodied family state,

01:52:34.640 --> 01:52:38.020
and a full question of one child policy.

01:52:38.020 --> 01:52:40.870
In fact, the most interesting
thing about one child policy

01:52:40.870 --> 01:52:43.970
was that is the continuation
of these austerity measures

01:52:43.970 --> 01:52:47.420
of family planning

01:52:47.420 --> 01:52:51.790
which started as part and parcel

01:52:51.790 --> 01:52:55.270
of this austerity measures in the 60s

01:52:55.270 --> 01:52:58.070
that were actually put forward

01:52:58.070 --> 01:53:01.310
to deal with the fallout
of Great Leap Forward.

01:53:01.310 --> 01:53:04.100
So what happened was that
while all other measures

01:53:04.100 --> 01:53:07.673
cease to practice because they
were extremely ideological,

01:53:08.700 --> 01:53:12.117
more ideological, ideologically embodied,

01:53:12.117 --> 01:53:15.133
this one child policy,
this family planning,

01:53:16.800 --> 01:53:19.880
were only strengthened and
indeed institutionalized

01:53:19.880 --> 01:53:22.510
to it's extreme form as one child policy

01:53:22.510 --> 01:53:24.690
in post Mao's China.

01:53:24.690 --> 01:53:28.180
So what happened was that
there was a change in context

01:53:28.180 --> 01:53:32.030
and political interpretations
of this policy

01:53:32.030 --> 01:53:34.960
from a anti-crisis measure,

01:53:34.960 --> 01:53:38.020
so from austerity measure

01:53:38.020 --> 01:53:41.710
to a new discourse of state development.

01:53:41.710 --> 01:53:43.760
So basically, it actually helps.

01:53:43.760 --> 01:53:45.680
I mean, so the basic justification

01:53:45.680 --> 01:53:47.640
was that having less babies,

01:53:47.640 --> 01:53:50.810
having less child was a
boon to the family welfare

01:53:50.810 --> 01:53:52.020
because to reduce the cost

01:53:52.020 --> 01:53:54.530
and eventually will lead to
better economic development

01:53:54.530 --> 01:53:55.880
that will benefit families.

01:53:56.970 --> 01:54:00.730
So it very much ties within
this big concept Xiaokang

01:54:00.730 --> 01:54:03.920
as modernization economic development,

01:54:03.920 --> 01:54:05.100
but it was not initially so

01:54:05.100 --> 01:54:08.970
when it was first
conceptualized in the 60s.

01:54:08.970 --> 01:54:09.803
Thank you.

01:54:13.530 --> 01:54:18.030
- Okay, thank you, Denise,
for the excellent question.

01:54:20.250 --> 01:54:23.133
I'll go relatively quickly
I know we're short on time.

01:54:26.300 --> 01:54:27.840
Basically there's two agencies

01:54:27.840 --> 01:54:31.430
that are sort of responsible for tourism,

01:54:31.430 --> 01:54:35.410
for inbound foreign tourism
in China during this period,

01:54:35.410 --> 01:54:37.790
one is the China
International Travel Service,

01:54:37.790 --> 01:54:41.410
which hosts quote, unquote,
sort of foreign tourists,

01:54:41.410 --> 01:54:44.930
The other is the Overseas
Chinese Travel Service

01:54:44.930 --> 01:54:49.410
which hosts musically overseas
Chinese and many Hong Kong

01:54:49.410 --> 01:54:50.930
and Macau compatriots.

01:54:50.930 --> 01:54:52.930
Of course, not every tourist to China

01:54:52.930 --> 01:54:54.480
comes through one of these agencies,

01:54:54.480 --> 01:54:58.660
there's a variety of organizations
in China during this time

01:54:58.660 --> 01:55:01.923
that host foreigners and
take them to sightsee.

01:55:03.050 --> 01:55:04.870
One thing that I think I wanna underscore,

01:55:04.870 --> 01:55:07.970
particularly regarding the
economic aspects of tourism,

01:55:07.970 --> 01:55:10.970
is that for every tourists hosted

01:55:10.970 --> 01:55:13.070
by the China International Travel Service,

01:55:13.070 --> 01:55:16.290
basically every sort
of European, Japanese,

01:55:16.290 --> 01:55:17.793
other Western tourists,

01:55:19.120 --> 01:55:21.830
there are probably 10 tourists

01:55:21.830 --> 01:55:24.127
hosted by the Overseas
Chinese Travel Service.

01:55:24.127 --> 01:55:26.720
And so economically, the
Overseas Chinese Travel Service

01:55:26.720 --> 01:55:29.100
is always the more important agency

01:55:29.100 --> 01:55:32.780
even though in a lot of the
sort of the travelers accounts

01:55:32.780 --> 01:55:34.290
we see a lot more about

01:55:34.290 --> 01:55:35.980
the China International Travel Service,

01:55:35.980 --> 01:55:37.460
and particularly, we see a lot

01:55:37.460 --> 01:55:40.580
about the sort of the
frustrations about how this agency

01:55:40.580 --> 01:55:41.790
it sort of prevents people

01:55:41.790 --> 01:55:45.400
from traveling to places they want to go.

01:55:45.400 --> 01:55:48.470
But all that being said

01:55:48.470 --> 01:55:50.220
despite having these sort of two

01:55:50.220 --> 01:55:51.780
sort of overarching agencies,

01:55:51.780 --> 01:55:54.370
their operations are
very, very decentralized,

01:55:54.370 --> 01:55:58.980
and so they're each branch
office in a particular city

01:55:58.980 --> 01:56:01.670
is really sort of staffed locally

01:56:01.670 --> 01:56:05.970
and is sort of taking some
sort of broad direction

01:56:05.970 --> 01:56:06.870
from the head office,

01:56:06.870 --> 01:56:09.090
but it's really like it's up to themselves

01:56:09.090 --> 01:56:11.220
to sort of like manage their finances

01:56:11.220 --> 01:56:13.330
and manage their program.

01:56:13.330 --> 01:56:16.250
And so that's why I think we
see in the Cultural Revolution,

01:56:16.250 --> 01:56:18.830
and I think this sort of is how I'll segue

01:56:18.830 --> 01:56:20.970
to, I think, Sarah, China's question

01:56:20.970 --> 01:56:23.010
about the periodization.

01:56:23.010 --> 01:56:26.800
In 1969, the head
offices of these agencies

01:56:26.800 --> 01:56:30.070
get shut down in Beijing but
the branches still continue,

01:56:30.070 --> 01:56:31.620
so the branches are still operating

01:56:31.620 --> 01:56:33.480
throughout the Cultural Revolution.

01:56:33.480 --> 01:56:36.330
And so in 1971 when there's
this sort of decision

01:56:36.330 --> 01:56:37.190
from top leadership

01:56:37.190 --> 01:56:39.923
that we can make some money from tourism,

01:56:40.800 --> 01:56:43.840
the branches are really pretty quickly

01:56:43.840 --> 01:56:48.840
able to sort of to kick to
step into gear and host people.

01:56:49.800 --> 01:56:53.840
And I mean, I think part
of that decision in 1971,

01:56:53.840 --> 01:56:55.640
it's a very practical decision,

01:56:55.640 --> 01:56:58.680
but I think it's also related
to the Cultural Revolution.

01:56:58.680 --> 01:57:01.270
We've seen that we've
hosted a lot of people,

01:57:01.270 --> 01:57:03.360
the Chinese government says
we've hosted a lot of people

01:57:03.360 --> 01:57:04.850
to come during the Cultural Revolution

01:57:04.850 --> 01:57:08.630
and people are really drawn
to China during that period,

01:57:08.630 --> 01:57:11.930
they want to see the
revolution that's going on.

01:57:11.930 --> 01:57:13.670
And so this is a chance finally

01:57:15.560 --> 01:57:17.500
that there's a sort of real realization,

01:57:17.500 --> 01:57:20.150
we don't have to foot the
bill for all these people,

01:57:20.150 --> 01:57:22.080
a lot of these people are willing to come

01:57:22.080 --> 01:57:23.840
and pay their own way

01:57:23.840 --> 01:57:28.770
and this is a chance for us
to sort of to make some money

01:57:28.770 --> 01:57:30.030
from hosting these people.

01:57:30.030 --> 01:57:31.390
So there's always a political element

01:57:31.390 --> 01:57:32.720
and an economic element,

01:57:32.720 --> 01:57:34.450
but there's this realization in 1971

01:57:34.450 --> 01:57:36.650
that we can earn some money

01:57:36.650 --> 01:57:39.100
even as we sort of educate people

01:57:39.100 --> 01:57:42.387
as to sort of what our vision
of sort of socialism is,

01:57:42.387 --> 01:57:44.633
and what our vision of the new China is.

01:57:48.290 --> 01:57:49.800
- Thank you for that.

01:57:49.800 --> 01:57:51.491
so we are at time,

01:57:51.491 --> 01:57:54.870
but I've been corresponding
with the panelists in the chat

01:57:54.870 --> 01:57:57.120
and I think that consensus is we're okay

01:57:57.120 --> 01:57:58.470
with going a few minutes over

01:57:58.470 --> 01:58:00.410
since there's still a number of questions

01:58:00.410 --> 01:58:02.290
that didn't get addressed.

01:58:02.290 --> 01:58:05.640
So if those of you who need
to go in the participants

01:58:05.640 --> 01:58:06.473
are welcome to go,

01:58:06.473 --> 01:58:09.010
but I think as a chair I'll
exercise that prerogative

01:58:09.010 --> 01:58:11.250
and extend us for about 10 minutes

01:58:11.250 --> 01:58:13.750
since we're not at HA,

01:58:13.750 --> 01:58:15.660
no one is going to push
us out of the room,

01:58:15.660 --> 01:58:19.523
and this is Hernanda to get
us all together in one space.

01:58:20.500 --> 01:58:24.960
So perhaps we can do one more
question or one more comment

01:58:24.960 --> 01:58:27.860
from each panelist as
a way of wrapping up.

01:58:27.860 --> 01:58:29.590
Adam, you have a rather long list

01:58:29.590 --> 01:58:31.920
of five or six other questions
that haven't gotten answered.

01:58:31.920 --> 01:58:35.040
So if you wanna choose one
or two of those to address

01:58:35.040 --> 01:58:40.040
and a final concluding remark,
you're welcome to do that.

01:58:40.180 --> 01:58:43.310
Yanjie, you got a question in the chat

01:58:43.310 --> 01:58:45.960
that I know you answered
to the individual,

01:58:45.960 --> 01:58:48.203
but maybe you could
answer it to the group.

01:58:50.190 --> 01:58:53.300
Mark Levine asks, did you
come across any instances

01:58:53.300 --> 01:58:56.430
where sent-down youths
wanted to stay in rural areas

01:58:56.430 --> 01:58:58.150
contrary to the majority?

01:58:58.150 --> 01:59:00.180
What reasons would have considered

01:59:00.180 --> 01:59:01.360
contributed to this desire,

01:59:01.360 --> 01:59:03.563
ideological, familial, et cetera?

01:59:06.150 --> 01:59:07.640
Gavin, I think you've just answered

01:59:07.640 --> 01:59:09.840
Sarah's question verbally.

01:59:09.840 --> 01:59:11.240
So if there's anything
else you want to add

01:59:11.240 --> 01:59:14.350
by way of conclusion, please do so.

01:59:14.350 --> 01:59:19.350
And then, Peter, I think I'm not seeing

01:59:19.530 --> 01:59:21.680
a final wrap up question
for you in the Q&amp;A

01:59:23.460 --> 01:59:27.990
so maybe I can ask a final
question for your paper.

01:59:27.990 --> 01:59:31.770
As you were showing all of those
screenshots of the campuses

01:59:31.770 --> 01:59:33.360
and that people standing around,

01:59:33.360 --> 01:59:36.530
I was thinking about
the architecture of MIT

01:59:36.530 --> 01:59:38.249
and the architecture of Tsinghua,

01:59:38.249 --> 01:59:41.540
so they're based on each other as a model.

01:59:41.540 --> 01:59:45.060
And so I wondered about
longer institutional links,

01:59:45.060 --> 01:59:50.060
was this something that
was either called upon

01:59:50.510 --> 01:59:52.070
in the building of these schools,

01:59:52.070 --> 01:59:56.240
or if these longer institutional links

01:59:56.240 --> 01:59:58.430
were something that were invoked

01:59:58.430 --> 02:00:00.130
in the commemorative materials

02:00:00.130 --> 02:00:02.060
that you were using in your research.

02:00:02.060 --> 02:00:04.330
Another way of asking this question is

02:00:04.330 --> 02:00:06.490
in your paper in the presentation,

02:00:06.490 --> 02:00:08.790
you looked at the Hong Kong alums

02:00:08.790 --> 02:00:10.530
and these Chinese American alums,

02:00:10.530 --> 02:00:15.280
were there any Chinese alums
of an earlier generation

02:00:15.280 --> 02:00:20.200
that were part of this MBA
or the management education

02:00:20.200 --> 02:00:21.850
as you've started to research it?

02:00:26.460 --> 02:00:28.330
- Do you want me to go then in that order?

02:00:28.330 --> 02:00:29.720
Okay, sure.

02:00:29.720 --> 02:00:31.990
Yeah, so I think there's
a lot of great questions.

02:00:31.990 --> 02:00:34.410
I'm sorry, I can't answer them all.

02:00:34.410 --> 02:00:37.510
I think I'll answer Ron Byrd's question

02:00:37.510 --> 02:00:41.240
and he asks, from an archival concern,

02:00:41.240 --> 02:00:43.180
what has been the fate of
these documents you discovered

02:00:43.180 --> 02:00:44.013
on your research?

02:00:44.013 --> 02:00:47.160
Is there a common fate
of PRC archival documents

02:00:47.160 --> 02:00:50.280
to be discovered in private hands?

02:00:50.280 --> 02:00:51.170
Great question.

02:00:51.170 --> 02:00:54.950
So there's this whole kind
of school of literature

02:00:54.950 --> 02:00:59.000
that is sometimes referred
to as sinological garbology,

02:00:59.000 --> 02:01:01.150
although I don't really like that term.

02:01:01.150 --> 02:01:04.070
But it was really I
think Michael Schoenhals

02:01:04.070 --> 02:01:06.780
was really one of the kind
of leaders in this field,

02:01:06.780 --> 02:01:09.160
and beginning in the 1990s
he started going around

02:01:09.160 --> 02:01:11.310
to these flea markets just as I've done,

02:01:11.310 --> 02:01:15.780
and buying a lot of
former government archives

02:01:15.780 --> 02:01:18.240
that had slipped into private circulation.

02:01:18.240 --> 02:01:20.960
So during the 1980s in China,

02:01:20.960 --> 02:01:23.380
there was this massive purge of archives

02:01:23.380 --> 02:01:26.850
as the various government
institutions were being merged,

02:01:26.850 --> 02:01:31.710
and at the time, there were
paper shortages in China.

02:01:31.710 --> 02:01:35.970
So instead of just burning the
documents or scrapping them,

02:01:35.970 --> 02:01:39.660
the archivists were instructed
to turn over these documents

02:01:39.660 --> 02:01:42.880
to paper pulpers who would
theoretically recycle them,

02:01:42.880 --> 02:01:44.900
but then somewhere between the archivist

02:01:44.900 --> 02:01:45.920
and the paper pulper,

02:01:45.920 --> 02:01:50.050
someone decides to like sell
them into private circulation.

02:01:50.050 --> 02:01:52.830
So there are enormous quantities

02:01:52.830 --> 02:01:55.770
of local government archival materials

02:01:55.770 --> 02:01:58.140
still in private circulation today.

02:01:58.140 --> 02:02:02.170
And you see many scholars
and institutions now,

02:02:02.170 --> 02:02:04.380
particularly because of the
recent archival closures

02:02:04.380 --> 02:02:06.610
in China in the past few years,

02:02:06.610 --> 02:02:07.774
there's been a lot of people

02:02:07.774 --> 02:02:11.280
that have been working to
recollect these documents

02:02:11.280 --> 02:02:14.540
and then assemble private
and institutional archives.

02:02:14.540 --> 02:02:18.870
So Yanje is a prime example,
the archive he worked with,

02:02:18.870 --> 02:02:20.940
is at Fudan started by John Latian,

02:02:20.940 --> 02:02:25.040
it's a really massive collection
of all different kinds

02:02:25.040 --> 02:02:26.740
of really interesting materials

02:02:26.740 --> 02:02:29.560
that he acquired from local villages

02:02:29.560 --> 02:02:33.363
in the Guangdong and Guangzhou area.

02:02:35.360 --> 02:02:36.680
But then there's scholars in Europe

02:02:36.680 --> 02:02:38.070
that are doing the same thing,

02:02:38.070 --> 02:02:41.170
Michael Schoenhals, Daniel
Lees, in particular,

02:02:41.170 --> 02:02:44.520
are leading this Stanford University

02:02:44.520 --> 02:02:45.610
and the Hoover Institute

02:02:45.610 --> 02:02:48.230
are trying to actively
procure these materials.

02:02:48.230 --> 02:02:50.130
So there is a collective effort

02:02:50.130 --> 02:02:53.910
among certain persuasion
of historians today

02:02:53.910 --> 02:02:58.910
to go and reconstitute
these local archives

02:02:59.360 --> 02:03:01.960
in order that we can continue to promote

02:03:01.960 --> 02:03:03.110
future historical research

02:03:03.110 --> 02:03:06.030
in the face of shrinking archival access.

02:03:06.030 --> 02:03:08.140
But as Yanjie mentions, there's
ethical concerns with this,

02:03:08.140 --> 02:03:09.870
how do we do this appropriately?

02:03:09.870 --> 02:03:13.500
How do we protect the
source of our sources?

02:03:13.500 --> 02:03:16.210
How do we protect the
privacy of the individuals

02:03:16.210 --> 02:03:17.750
mentioned in the sources?

02:03:17.750 --> 02:03:19.650
And I think for all of these concerns,

02:03:19.650 --> 02:03:21.670
personally, I think we
should adopt the practice

02:03:21.670 --> 02:03:24.140
of like anthropologists and journalists

02:03:24.140 --> 02:03:25.970
and just go for full anonymity

02:03:25.970 --> 02:03:29.430
and like we need to
rethink how as historians

02:03:29.430 --> 02:03:31.160
we engage in citation practices,

02:03:31.160 --> 02:03:33.760
how we make these materials
available to other people,

02:03:33.760 --> 02:03:36.930
but for example, I myself
am trying to fully digitize

02:03:36.930 --> 02:03:39.690
my collection and then make it available

02:03:39.690 --> 02:03:41.100
to the academic community

02:03:41.100 --> 02:03:45.270
after some kind of
privatization of the materials

02:03:45.270 --> 02:03:48.200
to protect the anonymity
of the people mentioned.

02:03:48.200 --> 02:03:50.210
But I think this is a
really important effort,

02:03:50.210 --> 02:03:52.610
and I hope other historians will join in

02:03:52.610 --> 02:03:56.013
and make it possible to continue
preserving these documents.

02:04:01.950 --> 02:04:04.370
- Just few words in addition to you items,

02:04:04.370 --> 02:04:08.060
I totally agree with you about
the methodology challenges,

02:04:08.060 --> 02:04:11.950
actually, we are, I mean, with
my collaborators in China,

02:04:11.950 --> 02:04:14.210
we are also digitizing
the letter collections

02:04:14.210 --> 02:04:18.080
and trying to make it into a textual form

02:04:18.080 --> 02:04:20.390
where we can actually use textual analysis

02:04:20.390 --> 02:04:23.910
or other more modern implements

02:04:23.910 --> 02:04:28.090
even like machine learning
to study the Roderick

02:04:28.090 --> 02:04:31.590
and the network's there.

02:04:31.590 --> 02:04:33.820
So there are a lot of things going on.

02:04:33.820 --> 02:04:37.930
I think we can have some exchanges later

02:04:37.930 --> 02:04:39.550
on the latest developments.

02:04:39.550 --> 02:04:41.610
So basically, I think grassroots stores

02:04:41.610 --> 02:04:44.330
will be increasingly
important for our research,

02:04:44.330 --> 02:04:46.920
and there are a lot of things on the table

02:04:46.920 --> 02:04:50.670
for us to think about ranging
from privacy protection

02:04:50.670 --> 02:04:54.090
to the kind of methodology
challenges of using them.

02:04:54.090 --> 02:04:55.870
So for Mao's question

02:04:55.870 --> 02:05:00.263
about who are those staying
behind sent-down use,

02:05:01.900 --> 02:05:03.357
actually there are a number of them

02:05:03.357 --> 02:05:06.570
but they were among the
very distinctive minority,

02:05:06.570 --> 02:05:10.170
and eventually, they were
all urbanized, I think,

02:05:10.170 --> 02:05:12.460
as far as I know but locally,

02:05:12.460 --> 02:05:16.100
so basically they moved
to the local counties

02:05:16.100 --> 02:05:20.180
or local cities rather than
going back to like big cities

02:05:20.180 --> 02:05:23.557
like Shanghai and Beijing where
they were originated from,

02:05:23.557 --> 02:05:26.210
and the big reason for
them to settle locally,

02:05:26.210 --> 02:05:28.290
and I happen to discuss about this

02:05:28.290 --> 02:05:31.860
in one of my dissertation
chapters about family reunion

02:05:31.860 --> 02:05:34.650
was that they already
started their families,

02:05:34.650 --> 02:05:37.710
and in the 70s, they
already started dating

02:05:37.710 --> 02:05:39.410
and that was a big issue

02:05:39.410 --> 02:05:41.350
because once she started that,

02:05:41.350 --> 02:05:42.990
you basically embedded locally

02:05:42.990 --> 02:05:46.470
and it's extremely difficult to extricate,

02:05:46.470 --> 02:05:47.990
that's what they use.

02:05:47.990 --> 02:05:50.730
And eventually, you have
to like forcefully lay down

02:05:50.730 --> 02:05:54.700
your own plan and thought
more about your children,

02:05:54.700 --> 02:05:57.240
and eventually, what happened was that

02:05:57.240 --> 02:05:58.610
you are bounded there.

02:05:58.610 --> 02:06:01.790
So that's what happened to most youth

02:06:01.790 --> 02:06:03.910
who eventually settle down locally

02:06:03.910 --> 02:06:06.283
and to become part of local society.

02:06:08.260 --> 02:06:09.940
I think that's all I can offer

02:06:11.497 --> 02:06:13.093
and thank you for the question.

02:06:15.080 --> 02:06:18.050
- Gavin, do you have any
final questions or comments

02:06:18.050 --> 02:06:20.293
you'd like to say to wrap up?

02:06:21.380 --> 02:06:23.020
- I think one thing I'd like to talk about

02:06:23.020 --> 02:06:27.600
is that you mentioned
sort of geographic aspect

02:06:27.600 --> 02:06:28.750
of my research,

02:06:28.750 --> 02:06:31.660
and I'd like to just talk about that,

02:06:31.660 --> 02:06:34.100
and going forward is that here,

02:06:34.100 --> 02:06:35.580
I think one sort of like

02:06:36.734 --> 02:06:38.960
one thing we see going
throughout this is that

02:06:38.960 --> 02:06:43.030
tourism is dependent on sort
of mobility and immobility.

02:06:43.030 --> 02:06:45.570
And in this case, in sort of my research,

02:06:45.570 --> 02:06:48.800
we see sort of the mobility of
people coming from Hong Kong

02:06:48.800 --> 02:06:50.460
or coming from other overseas

02:06:51.423 --> 02:06:54.180
to visit an immobile sort
of population in China

02:06:54.180 --> 02:06:55.700
during the 1970s.

02:06:55.700 --> 02:06:57.760
But of course now, I
mean, tourism in China

02:06:57.760 --> 02:06:59.230
is very, very different.

02:06:59.230 --> 02:07:02.271
The people studying tourism
and the students that I met

02:07:02.271 --> 02:07:05.050
when I was in China who
are studying tourism

02:07:05.050 --> 02:07:07.170
are not studying inbound foreign tourism,

02:07:07.170 --> 02:07:10.150
they're studying outbound tourism

02:07:10.150 --> 02:07:12.070
or they're studying domestic tourism.

02:07:12.070 --> 02:07:15.940
And so in my research
I see tourists coming

02:07:15.940 --> 02:07:19.140
and mainly centered on
Guangdong, Shanghai,

02:07:19.140 --> 02:07:20.713
Guangho and Beijing,

02:07:21.945 --> 02:07:23.690
and that sort of shifts in the 80s

02:07:23.690 --> 02:07:27.350
to match sort of broader
array of destinations

02:07:27.350 --> 02:07:29.110
are open to people,

02:07:29.110 --> 02:07:30.560
and part of that is by state decree,

02:07:30.560 --> 02:07:32.050
there's a certain destinations

02:07:32.050 --> 02:07:35.000
that are open to people
now like many, many more,

02:07:35.000 --> 02:07:38.850
but tourism today is
not about so much about

02:07:38.850 --> 02:07:40.570
bringing foreign currency from outside

02:07:40.570 --> 02:07:43.540
to sort of Guangdong and coastal China,

02:07:43.540 --> 02:07:46.780
it's about taking the money
and the mobility of people

02:07:46.780 --> 02:07:49.120
in Guangdong, and people
in Shanghai, and Beijing,

02:07:49.120 --> 02:07:50.740
and coastal urban areas

02:07:50.740 --> 02:07:54.440
and having them shift some of that income

02:07:54.440 --> 02:07:56.410
to help develop sort of the hinterland,

02:07:56.410 --> 02:08:00.110
whether it's Tibet, it's
Guangzhou, it's places like that.

02:08:00.110 --> 02:08:03.050
So I think tourism sort
of always functions

02:08:03.050 --> 02:08:06.607
with that tension of the
mobility of one population

02:08:06.607 --> 02:08:08.350
and the immobility of another,

02:08:08.350 --> 02:08:11.000
but it's a way of trying to sort of shift

02:08:11.880 --> 02:08:15.763
the flow of currency of some
sort from one place to another.

02:08:17.093 --> 02:08:18.093
- Thank you.

02:08:20.020 --> 02:08:21.313
Peter, last word.

02:08:24.530 --> 02:08:27.280
- You asked about the
architecture and the pairing

02:08:27.280 --> 02:08:32.280
between MIT and Tsinghua as
you correctly diagnosed there,

02:08:33.560 --> 02:08:38.560
that it was very much evoked
in this kind of superficial way

02:08:38.860 --> 02:08:42.550
as this logical pairing makes
a lot of historical sense

02:08:42.550 --> 02:08:44.030
because of our previous roots,

02:08:44.030 --> 02:08:46.589
and in some sense, almost a healing

02:08:46.589 --> 02:08:50.520
of re-establishing these
ties and these connections.

02:08:50.520 --> 02:08:54.640
But as you sharply picked
up on part of my interest

02:08:54.640 --> 02:08:56.540
and entry into this

02:08:56.540 --> 02:08:59.250
was through the Hong
Kong elite connection,

02:08:59.250 --> 02:09:03.250
and that people of the foreign tycoons

02:09:03.250 --> 02:09:04.570
that I pictured there,

02:09:04.570 --> 02:09:07.120
two of them, either their
father or grandfather

02:09:07.120 --> 02:09:10.300
had gone to Tsinghua as
boxer identity students

02:09:10.300 --> 02:09:12.123
and then on to MIT.

02:09:13.570 --> 02:09:18.570
And that as very savvy operators

02:09:19.210 --> 02:09:23.860
who would understand that
education is training

02:09:23.860 --> 02:09:26.790
and that the kind of social capital

02:09:26.790 --> 02:09:30.470
you can buy through
donations to institutions,

02:09:30.470 --> 02:09:32.710
their agenda, I think, is
really interesting here

02:09:32.710 --> 02:09:36.240
at a more critical and political reading

02:09:39.150 --> 02:09:40.170
of what they're doing

02:09:40.170 --> 02:09:42.730
rather than just the celebratory accounts

02:09:42.730 --> 02:09:47.400
of multi-generational
educational patronage,

02:09:47.400 --> 02:09:52.380
that instead kind of trying
to steer educational systems

02:09:52.380 --> 02:09:56.570
in the mainland toward what
they think would be useful

02:09:56.570 --> 02:09:59.423
in the future, in most
especially to themselves.

02:10:02.990 --> 02:10:06.130
This is a somewhat roundabout way

02:10:06.130 --> 02:10:08.130
of saying that part of my interest here

02:10:08.130 --> 02:10:12.960
I think is I find MIT's
agenda absolutely baffling.

02:10:12.960 --> 02:10:16.040
I find it fascinating
that they pursued this

02:10:16.040 --> 02:10:18.500
in the way that they did
largely on pretty vague ideas

02:10:18.500 --> 02:10:21.773
of what they were gonna get
out of it in terms of prestige.

02:10:24.194 --> 02:10:25.850
But weird, interesting that I think

02:10:25.850 --> 02:10:28.300
that the Hong Kong elite donors agendas,

02:10:28.300 --> 02:10:33.300
they I get deeply anti-communist
and kind of very immersed

02:10:34.000 --> 02:10:36.530
in this kind of high
transactional politics

02:10:36.530 --> 02:10:39.090
of elite higher education
and what it can do

02:10:40.234 --> 02:10:42.323
if that answers your question.

02:10:43.260 --> 02:10:44.910
- Thank you.

02:10:44.910 --> 02:10:47.010
So, again, I want to wrap up

02:10:47.010 --> 02:10:49.660
and thank all of the panelists,

02:10:49.660 --> 02:10:51.530
to Yanjie for organizing,

02:10:51.530 --> 02:10:56.119
to Stephanie Kim and the Yale
Council of East Asian studies

02:10:56.119 --> 02:10:57.423
for hosting us today.

